DSP.mod(5,{"m5-descriptive":{"id":"m5-descriptive","slug":"descriptive-statistics","m":5,"title":"Descriptive statistics","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":[],"tags":["statistics","descriptive"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Measures of centre, spread, shape and association, including why sample variance divides by n-1, Pearson vs Spearman correlation, and the traps of correlation and aggregation.","keyTerms":["mean","median","mode","variance","standard deviation","Bessel's correction","IQR","skewness","z-score","covariance","correlation","Simpson's paradox"],"interviewQs":["Why do we divide by n-1 for sample variance?","When would you report the median instead of the mean?","Does zero correlation imply independence?","What is Simpson's paradox? Give an example."],"mistakes":["Reporting the mean of a heavily skewed variable","Saying correlation implies causation","Computing population variance when a sample variance is needed"],"practice":"Take a skewed variable (income, delivery time), compute mean, median, IQR and skewness, and explain which you would report and why.","related":["m5-probability","m3-viz","m8-preprocessing"],"points":"Concept - Key points\nMean, median, mode - Mean is sensitive to outliers; median robust. Right-skewed: mean > median > mode.\nVariance / SD - Population divides by N; sample divides by N-1 (Bessel's correction, because the sample mean is fitted from data).\nRange, IQR - IQR = Q3 - Q1; robust spread measure.\nSkewness - Asymmetry. Positive = long right tail. Fix with log/sqrt/Box-Cox.\nKurtosis - Tail heaviness.\nZ-score - (x - mean)/SD; number of SDs from the mean.\nCovariance - Direction of joint variation; scale-dependent.\nCorrelation - Standardized covariance in [-1, 1]. Pearson = linear; Spearman = monotonic (rank-based); Kendall = rank concordance.\nPercentile / quantile - Value below which p% of data fall.\nCoefficient of variation - SD / mean; compares spread across scales.\n\nInterview traps: correlation != causation; zero correlation does not mean independence (e.g., y = x^2); Simpson's paradox (aggregate trend reverses within subgroups)."},"intuitive":"<p>Descriptive statistics are the few numbers that describe a messy pile of data. The average (mean) is the fair share; the median is the person in the exact middle of the line; the spread tells you whether everyone is similar or wildly different. One billionaire walking into a village drags the average income up but does not change the median at all, which is why we report medians for things like income and delivery time. Correlation asks whether two things rise and fall together, but two things moving together does not mean one causes the other.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> These are the first questions in any stats screening round, and the traps (skew, Simpson&#39;s paradox, correlation vs causation) are favourites.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Pick the summary that fits the shape of the data, and never read causation into correlation.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Descriptive statistics summarise data with a centre, a spread and a shape. The mean is the average and is pulled by outliers; the median is the middle value and resists them; the mode is the most common. For spread, variance is the average squared distance from the mean, standard deviation is its square root in the original units, and the interquartile range is the middle fifty per cent. Skew says which tail is long: income is right-skewed, so mean exceeds median. Correlation measures linear association from minus one to plus one, and says nothing about causation. The catch: sample variance divides by n minus one, not n, to correct for estimating the mean from the same data.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"One extreme value against the mean, median and IQR","html":"<p>Monthly salaries (in thousands) at a six-person startup: 30, 35, 40, 45, 50 and the founder&#39;s 300.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Mean:</strong> (30 + 35 + 40 + 45 + 50 + 300) / 6 = 500 / 6 ≈ <strong>83.3</strong>. Five of the six people earn less than this &quot;average&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Median:</strong> sort and take the middle. With six values, average the 3rd and 4th: (40 + 45) / 2 = <strong>42.5</strong>. That is a typical salary.</li>\n<li><strong>Standard deviation:</strong> about <strong>106</strong>, larger than every salary except one. A single value inflated it, so here it does not describe the &quot;typical spread&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>A robust spread, the IQR.</strong> The quartiles are Q1 ≈ 36.25 and Q3 ≈ 48.75, so IQR = 12.5: the middle half of the team sits within 12.5 thousand of each other.</li>\n<li><strong>Flag the outlier with the IQR fences.</strong> Upper fence = Q3 + 1.5 × IQR = 48.75 + 18.75 = 67.5. The value 300 is far above it, so it is flagged. The lower fence is 36.25 − 18.75 = 17.5, and nobody is below it.</li>\n<li><strong>Report it honestly:</strong> &quot;The median salary is 42.5 thousand; one founder salary of 300 thousand raises the mean to 83.&quot;</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The mean and standard deviation follow extreme values; the median and IQR resist them. With skewed data, lead with the robust pair.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-centre-mean-median-mode","text":"Centre: mean, median, mode","level":3},{"id":"h-spread-variance-standard-deviation-iqr","text":"Spread: variance, standard deviation, IQR","level":3},{"id":"h-shape-skewness-and-kurtosis","text":"Shape: skewness and kurtosis","level":3},{"id":"h-position-z-scores-and-percentiles","text":"Position: z-scores and percentiles","level":3},{"id":"h-association-covariance-and-correlation","text":"Association: covariance and correlation","level":3},{"id":"h-simpson-s-paradox","text":"Simpson&#39;s paradox","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Key points</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mean, median, mode</td><td>Mean is sensitive to outliers; median robust. Right-skewed: mean &gt; median &gt; mode.</td></tr><tr><td>Variance / SD</td><td>Population divides by N; sample divides by N-1 (Bessel&#39;s correction, because the sample mean is fitted from data).</td></tr><tr><td>Range, IQR</td><td>IQR = Q3 - Q1; robust spread measure.</td></tr><tr><td>Skewness</td><td>Asymmetry. Positive = long right tail. Fix with log/sqrt/Box-Cox.</td></tr><tr><td>Kurtosis</td><td>Tail heaviness.</td></tr><tr><td>Z-score</td><td>(x - mean)/SD; number of SDs from the mean.</td></tr><tr><td>Covariance</td><td>Direction of joint variation; scale-dependent.</td></tr><tr><td>Correlation</td><td>Standardized covariance in [-1, 1]. Pearson = linear; Spearman = monotonic (rank-based); Kendall = rank concordance.</td></tr><tr><td>Percentile / quantile</td><td>Value below which p% of data fall.</td></tr><tr><td>Coefficient of variation</td><td>SD / mean; compares spread across scales.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Interview traps:</strong> correlation != causation; zero correlation does not mean independence (e.g., y = x^2); Simpson&#39;s paradox (aggregate trend reverses within subgroups).</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-centre-mean-median-mode\">Centre: mean, median, mode</h3>\n<p>Take six monthly incomes (in thousands of rupees): 20, 22, 25, 27, 30, 200.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mean = (20 + 22 + 25 + 27 + 30 + 200) / 6 = <strong>54</strong>. The 200 drags it far above almost everyone.</li>\n<li>Median = average of the middle two = (25 + 27) / 2 = <strong>26</strong>. It describes the typical person.</li>\n<li>The data is <strong>right-skewed</strong> (long tail to the right), so mean &gt; median &gt; mode, as in the course table.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The mean uses every value, is best for totals and symmetric data, but is sensitive to outliers. The median is robust. Use the median (and percentiles) for income, prices, delivery times, session lengths.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-spread-variance-standard-deviation-iqr\">Spread: variance, standard deviation, IQR</h3>\n<p><strong>Variance</strong> is the average squared distance from the mean; <strong>standard deviation (SD)</strong> is its square root, in the original units. <strong>IQR</strong> = Q3 − Q1 is the range of the middle 50% and is robust to outliers.</p>\n<p><strong>Why n − 1 for a sample (Bessel&#39;s correction).</strong> You usually do not know the true mean, so you use the sample mean, which is by construction the point closest to your own data. Distances to it are therefore slightly smaller than distances to the true mean, so dividing by n underestimates the true variance. Dividing by n − 1 corrects that exactly on average (it is <strong>unbiased</strong>). Another view: once the mean is fixed, only n − 1 of the deviations are free to vary (they must sum to zero), so there are n − 1 degrees of freedom.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\nx = np.array([<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">9</span>])\nx.var(), x.var(ddof=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># (4.0, 4.571...)   population vs sample variance</span>\nx.std(ddof=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)                 <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 2.138</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Check it by simulation: draw many samples of size 5 from a population with variance 4; the average of the <code>ddof=0</code> variances comes out near 3.2 (= 4 × 4/5) while the <code>ddof=1</code> version averages about 4.</p>\n<p><strong>Coefficient of variation</strong> = SD / mean compares spread across different scales (is delivery-time variation bigger than order-value variation?).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-shape-skewness-and-kurtosis\">Shape: skewness and kurtosis</h3>\n<p><strong>Skewness</strong> measures asymmetry: positive means a long right tail. Fix skewed positive data with a log, square-root or Box-Cox transform. <strong>Kurtosis</strong> measures tail heaviness (how prone the variable is to extreme values).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-position-z-scores-and-percentiles\">Position: z-scores and percentiles</h3>\n<p>A <strong>z-score</strong> is <code>(x − mean) / SD</code>: how many standard deviations from the mean. About 95% of normal data has |z| &lt; 2. A <strong>percentile</strong> is the value below which p% of the data fall; the median is the 50th.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-association-covariance-and-correlation\">Association: covariance and correlation</h3>\n<p><strong>Covariance</strong> shows whether two variables move together but depends on the units. <strong>Correlation</strong> rescales it to [−1, 1]. <strong>Pearson</strong> measures linear association; <strong>Spearman</strong> ranks the data first and so captures any monotonic relationship and resists outliers; <strong>Kendall</strong> counts concordant pairs.</p>\n<p>Play with the data: drag the relationship between the two variables and compare Pearson and Spearman, including the tricky shapes.</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"corr","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> scipy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> stats\nx = [<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>]; y = [<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">9</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">16</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">25</span>]\nstats.pearsonr(x, y)[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>]       <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 0.981  strong but not perfect: the curve is not a straight line</span>\nstats.spearmanr(x, y)[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>]      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 1.0    perfectly monotonic</span>\nx = np.array([-<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, -<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>]); np.corrcoef(x, x**<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>)[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>]    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 0.0  y = x^2 is perfectly dependent, yet r = 0</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout trap\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Correlation is not causation, and zero is not independence</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p>Ice-cream sales and drowning deaths correlate because both rise in summer (a <strong>confounder</strong>). And <code>y = x²</code> has correlation 0 with x despite complete dependence. Establish causation with randomised experiments or careful causal methods.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-simpson-s-paradox\">Simpson&#39;s paradox</h3>\n<p>A trend in the combined data can reverse inside every subgroup when group sizes are unbalanced. The classic case: a treatment looks worse overall but better in every subgroup, because the treatment was given mostly to the hard cases. Toggle the view below:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"simpson","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<p>The fix is to <strong>segment</strong> and compare like with like (or adjust for the confounding variable) before drawing conclusions. In product analytics: a feature can look better overall yet worse on both mobile and desktop if the mix of users changed.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-descriptive~1","q":"The mean delivery time is 38 minutes and the median is 31. What does this tell you, and what do you report to operations?","a":"<p>The mean exceeding the median indicates right skew: most deliveries are faster than 38 minutes, but a tail of slow ones inflates the average. Report the <strong>median</strong> for the typical experience and a <strong>high percentile (P90 or P95)</strong> for the bad tail, because operations cares about the late deliveries customers complain about. If cost is driven by total minutes, the mean still matters.</p>\n","s":"The data is right-skewed: a few very slow deliveries raise the mean; report the median plus P90."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-descriptive~ca0_1","q":"Salaries 40, 45, 50, 55, 400 (k). Mean and median, and which do you report?","a":"<p>Mean 118k, median 50k. Report the median; the outlier drags the mean.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-descriptive~ca0_2","q":"Why divide by n - 1 for sample variance?","a":"<p>The sample mean is fitted to the same data, which makes deviations too small; dividing by n - 1 removes that bias (Bessel&#39;s correction).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-descriptive~ca0_3","q":"Correlation is 0.0 between x and y. Does that mean they are unrelated?","a":"<p>No: it only rules out a linear relationship. y = x² has strong dependence and correlation near zero.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m5-descriptive~ta1_1","q":"<p>x takes the values −2, −1, 0, 1, 2 and y = x². Without a calculator, what is the correlation between x and y, and what does that teach you?</p>\n","a":"<p>y is 4, 1, 0, 1, 4. As x goes from −2 to 0, y falls; from 0 to 2, y rises by exactly the same amounts. The ups and downs cancel, so the correlation is exactly 0, even though y is completely determined by x. Correlation measures only straight-line association, so a correlation of 0 does not mean &quot;no relationship&quot;. Always plot the data.</p>\n"}]},"m5-probability":{"id":"m5-probability","slug":"probability-fundamentals","m":5,"title":"Probability fundamentals","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":[],"tags":["statistics","probability"],"qa":6,"ai":{"scope":"Probability rules, independence vs mutual exclusivity, counting, conditional probability, expectation and variance, with worked examples.","keyTerms":["sample space","conditional probability","independence","mutually exclusive","law of total probability","combinations","expected value","linearity of expectation"],"interviewQs":["Are mutually exclusive events independent?","P(at least one six in four rolls)?","Explain linearity of expectation with an example."],"mistakes":["Treating mutually exclusive as independent","Multiplying probabilities that are not independent","Forgetting the complement trick"],"practice":"Solve ten small probability questions by writing the sample space, then check with a short simulation.","related":["m5-bayes","m5-distributions","m5-puzzles"],"points":"- Sample space, events, union/intersection, complement\n- Addition rule: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B)\n- Multiplication: P(A and B) = P(A) P(B|A)\n- Independence: P(A and B) = P(A)P(B); mutually exclusive is NOT the same as independent\n- Conditional probability: P(A|B) = P(A and B) / P(B)\n- Law of total probability: P(B) = sum over i of P(B|Ai) P(Ai)\n- Bayes' theorem: P(A|B) = P(B|A) P(A) / P(B)\n- Permutations (order matters) nPr = n!/(n-r)!; combinations nCr = n!/(r!(n-r)!)\n- Expected value E[X] = sum x P(x); linearity of expectation; variance Var(X) = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2"},"intuitive":"<p>Probability is a number between 0 (never) and 1 (always) that says how likely something is. To find it, count the ways you want to happen and divide by all the ways that could happen, as long as every way is equally likely. &quot;And&quot; usually means multiply (two coin flips both heads: 1/2 × 1/2). &quot;Or&quot; usually means add, but subtract the overlap so you do not count it twice. When something is hard to count directly, count its opposite and subtract from 1.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Probability puzzles and Bayes questions appear in almost every data science interview.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> List the sample space, use complements, and do not confuse &quot;cannot happen together&quot; with &quot;do not affect each other&quot;.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Probability is a number from zero to one for how likely an event is. For equally likely outcomes it is favourable divided by total. The addition rule: P of A or B equals P of A plus P of B minus P of both. The multiplication rule: P of A and B equals P of A times P of B given A. If events are independent, knowing one tells nothing about the other, so the product is simple. Conditional probability P of A given B restricts attention to the cases where B happened. &#39;At least one&#39; problems are easiest as one minus the chance of none. The catch: independent is not the same as mutually exclusive; exclusive events are strongly dependent, since one rules the other out.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Two dice, three questions","html":"<p>Roll two fair dice. There are 6 × 6 = 36 equally likely outcomes.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>P(sum = 7).</strong> Count the ways: (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), (6,1). That is 6 of 36 = <strong>1/6</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>P(at least one six).</strong> Counting directly is easy to get wrong, because (6,6) is easy to count twice. Use the complement: P(no six) = (5/6) × (5/6) = 25/36, so P(at least one six) = 1 − 25/36 = <strong>11/36 ≈ 0.31</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Check by counting:</strong> 6 outcomes with a six on the first die, 6 with a six on the second, minus (6,6) counted twice: 6 + 6 − 1 = 11. That is the addition rule, P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B).</li>\n<li><strong>P(sum = 8, given the first die shows 3).</strong> The condition shrinks the world to six outcomes: (3,1) to (3,6). Only (3,5) sums to 8, so the answer is <strong>1/6</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Independence.</strong> Knowing that the first die is 3 did not change the chance of a 5 on the second die. That is what independent means.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Count equally likely outcomes; for &quot;at least one&quot;, use one minus &quot;none&quot;; for &quot;given that&quot;, shrink the sample space before counting.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-core-rules-each-with-an-example","text":"The core rules, each with an example","level":3},{"id":"h-law-of-total-probability","text":"Law of total probability","level":3},{"id":"h-counting-permutations-and-combinations","text":"Counting: permutations and combinations","level":3},{"id":"h-expected-value-variance-and-linearity","text":"Expected value, variance and linearity","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li>Sample space, events, union/intersection, complement</li>\n<li>Addition rule: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A and B)</li>\n<li>Multiplication: P(A and B) = P(A) P(B|A)</li>\n<li>Independence: P(A and B) = P(A)P(B); mutually exclusive is NOT the same as independent</li>\n<li>Conditional probability: P(A|B) = P(A and B) / P(B)</li>\n<li><strong>Law of total probability:</strong> P(B) = sum over i of P(B|Ai) P(Ai)</li>\n<li><strong>Bayes&#39; theorem:</strong> P(A|B) = P(B|A) P(A) / P(B)</li>\n<li>Permutations (order matters) nPr = n!/(n-r)!; combinations nCr = n!/(r!(n-r)!)</li>\n<li>Expected value E[X] = sum x P(x); linearity of expectation; variance Var(X) = E[X^2] - (E[X])^2</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-core-rules-each-with-an-example\">The core rules, each with an example</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Complement.</strong> P(not A) = 1 − P(A). <em>At least one six in 4 rolls:</em> easier as 1 − P(no six in 4) = 1 − (5/6)⁴ = 1 − 625/1296 = <strong>0.518</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Addition.</strong> P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). P(heart or king) = 13/52 + 4/52 − 1/52 = 16/52.</li>\n<li><strong>Multiplication.</strong> P(A and B) = P(A) × P(B | A). Drawing two aces without replacement: 4/52 × 3/51 = 1/221.</li>\n<li><strong>Conditional.</strong> P(A | B) = P(A and B) / P(B): restrict attention to the world where B happened.</li>\n<li><strong>Independence.</strong> A and B are independent if P(A and B) = P(A) P(B), equivalently P(A | B) = P(A): learning B tells you nothing about A.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<aside class=\"callout trap\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Mutually exclusive is not independent</div><div class=\"callout-b\"><p>If A and B cannot both happen (P(A and B) = 0) and each has positive probability, then knowing A happened tells you B did <em>not</em>, so they are strongly <strong>dependent</strong>. Mutually exclusive events are independent only in the trivial case where one has probability 0.</p>\n</div></aside>"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<p><em>Checking independence:</em> roll two dice. A = &quot;first die is even&quot; (probability 1/2), B = &quot;sum is 7&quot; (6 of 36 = 1/6). A and B together: (2,5), (4,3), (6,1) = 3/36 = 1/12 = 1/2 × 1/6. So A and B are <strong>independent</strong>: a sum of 7 is equally likely whatever the first die shows.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-law-of-total-probability\">Law of total probability</h3>\n<p>If A₁, A₂, … partition the possibilities, then P(B) = Σ P(B | Aᵢ) P(Aᵢ). Example: 60% of orders come from Android (cancellation rate 5%) and 40% from iOS (cancellation rate 3%). Overall cancellation = 0.6 × 0.05 + 0.4 × 0.03 = <strong>4.2%</strong>. This is the denominator in Bayes&#39; theorem.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-counting-permutations-and-combinations\">Counting: permutations and combinations</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Permutations</strong> (order matters): nPr = n! / (n − r)!. Ways to award gold, silver, bronze among 10 runners: 10 × 9 × 8 = 720.</li>\n<li><strong>Combinations</strong> (order does not matter): nCr = n! / (r! (n − r)!). Choose 3 of 10 for a team: 120.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> math\nmath.perm(<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>), math.comb(<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>)       <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># (720, 120)</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-expected-value-variance-and-linearity\">Expected value, variance and linearity</h3>\n<p><strong>Expected value</strong> E[X] = Σ x · P(x) is the long-run average. <strong>Variance</strong> Var(X) = E[X²] − (E[X])².</p>\n<p><strong>Linearity of expectation:</strong> E[X + Y] = E[X] + E[Y], <em>always</em>, even when X and Y are dependent. It makes hard problems easy. Expected number of heads in 100 fair flips: 100 × 0.5 = 50, no distribution needed. Expected value of the maximum of two dice: E[max] = (1·1 + 2·3 + 3·5 + 4·7 + 5·9 + 6·11) / 36 = 161/36 ≈ <strong>4.47</strong> (the number of ways the max equals k is 2k − 1).</p>\n<p><strong>Simulate to check yourself:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\nrng = np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)\nrolls = rng.integers(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>, size=(<span class=\"hljs-number\">200_000</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>))\n(rolls == <span class=\"hljs-number\">6</span>).<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">any</span>(axis=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>).mean()        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># about 0.518: at least one six in four rolls</span>\nd = rng.integers(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">7</span>, size=(<span class=\"hljs-number\">200_000</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>))\nd.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">max</span>(axis=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>).mean()                   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># about 4.47</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-probability~1","q":"A test has 99% sensitivity. A patient tests positive. Is there a 99% chance they are sick?","a":"<p>The 99% is P(positive | sick). The question asks the reverse, P(sick | positive), which also needs the prevalence and the false-positive rate (Bayes&#39; theorem). With 1% prevalence and a 5% false-positive rate it is only about 16.7%. Confusing the two conditional probabilities is the <strong>base-rate fallacy</strong>; see the Bayes topic.</p>\n","s":"No: it depends on the base rate; P(sick | positive) is not P(positive | sick)."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-probability~2","q":"Two independent systems each fail with probability 0.1. What is the probability that at least one fails?","a":"<p>Use the complement: both work with probability 0.9 × 0.9 = 0.81, so at least one fails with 1 − 0.81 = <strong>0.19</strong>. Using P(A) + P(B) = 0.2 would overcount the case where both fail (0.01); the addition rule&#39;s subtraction term fixes it: 0.1 + 0.1 − 0.01 = 0.19.</p>\n","s":"1 − 0.9 × 0.9 = 0.19."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-probability~ca0_1","q":"Chance of at least one six in four rolls of a die?","a":"<p>1 - (5/6)^4 ≈ 0.518.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-probability~ca0_2","q":"Can two events with positive probability be both mutually exclusive and independent?","a":"<p>No. If exclusive, P(A and B) = 0, but independence requires P(A)P(B) &gt; 0.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-probability~ca0_3","q":"Two cards drawn without replacement: probability both are aces?","a":"<p>4/52 × 3/51 = 1/221 ≈ 0.45%.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m5-probability~ta1_1","q":"<p>Draw two cards from a shuffled 52-card deck without putting the first back. What is the probability that both are red? Why is it not (1/2) × (1/2)?</p>\n","a":"<p>P(first red) = 26/52. Given that, 25 red cards remain among 51, so P(second red | first red) = 25/51. Multiply: (26/52) × (25/51) ≈ 0.245. It is not 0.25 because the draws are not independent: taking a red card out leaves slightly fewer reds. With replacement, the answer would be exactly 0.25.</p>\n"}]},"m5-bayes":{"id":"m5-bayes","slug":"bayes-theorem-the-worked-example","m":5,"title":"Bayes' theorem: the worked example","min":25,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m5-probability"],"tags":["statistics","probability","bayes"],"qa":6,"ai":{"scope":"Bayes' theorem through the classic medical-test example, natural frequencies, sequential updating, and how the same logic applies to fraud and spam alerts.","keyTerms":["Bayes' theorem","prior","likelihood","posterior","sensitivity","false positive rate","base rate fallacy","natural frequencies"],"interviewQs":["A disease has 1% prevalence; the test has 99% sensitivity and 5% false positives. What is P(disease | positive)?","Why are most positive results false positives here?","How does a second positive test change the answer?"],"mistakes":["Confusing P(positive | disease) with P(disease | positive)","Ignoring the base rate","Forgetting P(positive) is the sum over both groups"],"practice":"Change the prevalence to 10% and recompute; then explain the change to a non-technical person.","related":["m5-probability","m7-naive-bayes","m8-imbalanced"],"points":"A disease has 1% prevalence. A test has 99% sensitivity and 5% false-positive rate. You test positive. What is P(disease | positive)?\n\nInsight: with a low base rate, most positives are false positives. The same logic applies to fraud detection alerts and spam filters."},"intuitive":"<p>Imagine 10,000 people. Only 100 are really sick. The test catches nearly all of them (99 positives). But among the 9,900 healthy people, 5% get a false alarm: 495 people. So there are 99 + 495 = 594 positives, and only 99 of them are actually sick. If you test positive, your chance of being sick is 99 out of 594, about one in six. The test is good, but the disease is rare, so false alarms from the big healthy group swamp the real cases.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> It is the most common probability question in interviews, and the same logic explains why fraud alerts and spam filters raise so many false alarms.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Rare things plus imperfect tests mean most alarms are false. Count people, not percentages.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Bayes&#39; theorem turns P of evidence given hypothesis into P of hypothesis given evidence: posterior equals likelihood times prior, divided by the total probability of the evidence. The classic lesson is the rare disease test. If one per cent have the disease, the test catches ninety-nine per cent of cases and false-alarms five per cent of the time, then among a thousand people about ten are sick and nine or ten test positive, but about fifty healthy people also test positive. So a positive result means roughly sixteen per cent, not ninety-nine. The catch: the prior matters, and people forget it, which is called base-rate neglect.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"A fraud alert, with natural frequencies","html":"<p>0.5% of transactions are fraud. The alert catches 90% of fraud and wrongly flags 2% of genuine transactions. An alert fires: how likely is it to be fraud?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Imagine 10,000 transactions.</strong> 0.5% are fraud: <strong>50</strong> fraud and <strong>9,950</strong> genuine.</li>\n<li><strong>Alerts on fraud:</strong> 90% of 50 = <strong>45</strong> true alerts.</li>\n<li><strong>Alerts on genuine transactions:</strong> 2% of 9,950 = <strong>199</strong> false alarms.</li>\n<li><strong>All alerts:</strong> 45 + 199 = <strong>244</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Of the alerts, the fraud share:</strong> 45 / 244 ≈ <strong>18%</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Same answer from the formula:</strong> P(fraud | alert) = (0.9 × 0.005) / (0.9 × 0.005 + 0.02 × 0.995) = 0.0045 / 0.0244 ≈ 0.184.</li>\n<li><strong>Read it.</strong> Although the alert is &quot;90% accurate&quot; on fraud, about 4 alerts in 5 are false, because genuine transactions vastly outnumber fraud.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> When the thing you look for is rare, false alarms from the large group dominate. Turn percentages into counts out of 10,000 and the answer becomes obvious.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-formula-and-why-it-works","text":"The formula, and why it works","level":3},{"id":"h-natural-frequencies-the-version-to-use-in-an-int","text":"Natural frequencies: the version to use in an interview","level":3},{"id":"h-a-second-independent-positive-test","text":"A second, independent positive test","level":3},{"id":"h-where-else-this-shows-up","text":"Where else this shows up","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>A disease has 1% prevalence. A test has 99% sensitivity and 5% false-positive rate. You test positive. What is P(disease | positive)?</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Text</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">P(+|D) = 0.99, P(D) = 0.01, P(+|not D) = 0.05\nP(+) = 0.99*0.01 + 0.05*0.99 = 0.0099 + 0.0495 = 0.0594\nP(D|+) = 0.0099 / 0.0594 = 16.7%</code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>Insight: with a low base rate, most positives are false positives. The same logic applies to fraud detection alerts and spam filters.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-formula-and-why-it-works\">The formula, and why it works</h3>\n<p>Bayes&#39; theorem: <strong>P(A | B) = P(B | A) · P(A) / P(B)</strong>, where P(B) comes from the law of total probability.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Prior</strong> P(D) = 0.01: how likely before seeing the test.</li>\n<li><strong>Likelihood</strong> P(+ | D) = 0.99 (sensitivity) and P(+ | not D) = 0.05 (false-positive rate).</li>\n<li><strong>Posterior</strong> P(D | +): how likely after seeing a positive result.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Text</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">P(+) = P(+|D)·P(D) + P(+|not D)·P(not D) = 0.99·0.01 + 0.05·0.99 = 0.0099 + 0.0495 = 0.0594\nP(D|+) = 0.0099 / 0.0594 = 0.1667  → 16.7%</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-natural-frequencies-the-version-to-use-in-an-int\">Natural frequencies: the version to use in an interview</h3>\n<p>Start with 10,000 people:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Test positive</th><th>Test negative</th><th>Total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sick</td><td>99</td><td>1</td><td>100</td></tr><tr><td>Healthy</td><td>495</td><td>9,405</td><td>9,900</td></tr><tr><td>Total</td><td>594</td><td>9,406</td><td>10,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>P(sick | positive) = 99 / 594 = 16.7%. Drawing this table takes 20 seconds and is hard to get wrong. Try other numbers:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"bayes","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-a-second-independent-positive-test\">A second, independent positive test</h3>\n<p>After one positive result the probability of disease is 16.7% (the <em>posterior</em> becomes the new <em>prior</em>). A second independent positive:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Text</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">P(D | ++) = 0.99² × 0.01 / (0.99² × 0.01 + 0.05² × 0.99) = 0.009801 / 0.012276 = 79.8%</code></pre></div>\n<p>Odds form: prior odds 1:99 × likelihood ratio 19.8 per positive test. After two: 1/99 × 19.8 × 19.8 ≈ 3.96 to 1, i.e. 79.8%. Repeating a test is why doctors order confirmatory tests after a screening positive.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-where-else-this-shows-up\">Where else this shows up</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>&quot;Disease&quot;</th><th>&quot;Positive test&quot;</th><th>Consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fraud detection</td><td>transaction is fraud (0.1%)</td><td>model flags it</td><td>most flagged transactions are legitimate unless precision is very high</td></tr><tr><td>Spam filter</td><td>email is spam</td><td>filter flags it</td><td>blocking a real email is costly, so precision matters</td></tr><tr><td>Anomaly detection</td><td>real incident</td><td>alert fires</td><td>alert fatigue if the false-positive rate is not tiny</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>This is why <strong>precision</strong> (P(actual positive | flagged)) depends on the class balance while sensitivity does not, and why imbalanced problems use PR-AUC instead of accuracy.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-bayes~1","q":"Redo the example with prevalence 10% instead of 1%. What is P(disease | positive)?","a":"<p>P(+) = 0.99 × 0.10 + 0.05 × 0.90 = 0.099 + 0.045 = 0.144. P(D | +) = 0.099 / 0.144 = <strong>68.75%</strong>. A tenfold higher base rate lifts the posterior from 16.7% to 68.8%, with the same test. The test did not change; the population did.</p>\n","s":"0.099 / (0.099 + 0.045) ≈ 68.8%."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-bayes~2","q":"A fraud model has 95% recall and a 1% false-positive rate. Fraud is 0.2% of transactions. Of the transactions it flags, what fraction is really fraud?","a":"<p>Per 100,000 transactions: 200 fraud, 99,800 legitimate. True positives = 0.95 × 200 = 190. False positives = 0.01 × 99,800 = 998. Precision = 190 / (190 + 998) = <strong>16%</strong>. Even a model that looks strong on recall and false-positive rate produces mostly false alarms when the positive class is rare, so the review workload and the threshold must be chosen with the base rate in mind.</p>\n","s":"About 16%."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-bayes~ca0_1","q":"Prevalence 1%, sensitivity 99%, false-positive rate 5%. P(disease | positive)?","a":"<p>0.0099 / (0.0099 + 0.0495) ≈ 16.7%.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-bayes~ca0_2","q":"Why is the posterior so much lower than the test&#39;s accuracy?","a":"<p>The disease is rare, so false positives from the large healthy group outnumber true positives (base-rate neglect).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-bayes~ca0_3","q":"Which two numbers describe a test&#39;s quality independently of prevalence?","a":"<p>Sensitivity (true positive rate) and specificity (true negative rate).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m5-bayes~ta1_1","q":"<p>A flagged transaction (fraud probability now 18.4%) goes through a second, independent check with the same 90% catch rate and 2% false-alarm rate, and is flagged again. What is the probability of fraud now?</p>\n","a":"<p>Use the old posterior as the new prior: P = (0.9 × 0.184) / (0.9 × 0.184 + 0.02 × 0.816) = 0.1656 / (0.1656 + 0.0163) ≈ 0.91. Two independent alerts take it from 0.5% to about 91%. This is Bayesian updating: each piece of evidence multiplies the odds by the likelihood ratio, here 0.9 / 0.02 = 45.</p>\n"}]},"m5-puzzles":{"id":"m5-puzzles","slug":"classic-probability-puzzles","m":5,"title":"Classic probability puzzles","min":30,"type":"practice","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m5-probability"],"tags":["statistics","probability","puzzles"],"qa":16,"ai":{"scope":"Classic probability puzzles with full reasoning, plus simulations you can run, and extra puzzles commonly asked in interviews.","keyTerms":["dice","geometric distribution","Monty Hall","birthday problem","biased coin","expected value","Von Neumann trick"],"interviewQs":["Explain the Monty Hall problem and why switching wins 2/3 of the time.","How many people make a shared birthday 50% likely?","How do you get a fair decision from a biased coin?"],"mistakes":["Answering 1/2 for the two-children problem without listing outcomes","Not stating assumptions (equally likely outcomes)","Giving the answer without a reason"],"practice":"Solve each puzzle aloud in under two minutes, then verify by simulation in Python.","related":["m5-probability","m5-bayes"],"points":"Q: Two dice rolled: P(sum = 7)?\nA: 6/36 = 1/6\n\nQ: Family has two children, at least one is a girl: P(both girls)?\nA: 1/3\n\nQ: Expected number of rolls of a fair die to get a 6?\nA: 6, geometric\n\nQ: Birthday problem: how many people make a shared birthday about 50% likely?\nA: 23 people give roughly a 50% chance that two share a birthday.\n\nQ: Monty Hall: should you switch doors, and what is the win probability if you do?\nA: Yes. Switching wins with probability 2/3 (staying wins only 1/3).\n\nQ: You flip a fair coin until first head. Expected flips?\nA: 2\n\nQ: Drawing 2 cards from a deck: P(both aces)?\nA: 4/52 x 3/51 = 1/221\n\nQ: A biased coin with P(H)=0.7: how do you make a fair decision?\nA: Flip twice: HT vs TH, discard HH/TT.\n\nQ: Expected value of a game: pay Rs 10, roll a die, win Rs 3 x face value. What is the expected value?\nA: E = 3 x 3.5 = 10.5; fair-plus"},"intuitive":"<p>Probability puzzles are small stories where your gut is often wrong, which is the point. The trick is always the same: list all the equally-likely possibilities, cross out the ones that do not fit what you were told, and count what is left. In Monty Hall, your first pick is right only one time in three, and that does not change when the host opens a door, so the remaining door carries the other two-thirds.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers use these to see how you reason under pressure, not whether you memorised answers.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> List the possibilities, apply the information, count, then check with a simulation.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Probability puzzles reward a clean method. Define the sample space, count favourable outcomes, and check by simulation if unsure. Birthday problem: with 23 people the chance of a shared birthday passes fifty per cent, because we compare all pairs, about 253 of them. Monty Hall: switching wins two thirds of the time, since your first pick had one third and the host&#39;s reveal moves the other two thirds to the remaining door. Expected value problems use linearity: add up the expected parts, even when they are dependent. The catch: say the assumptions out loud, such as the host always opening a goat door, because the answer depends on them.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Monty Hall by listing every case","html":"<p>A car is behind one of three doors. You pick door 1. The host, who knows where the car is, opens another door that has a goat, then offers a switch.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>List the equally likely positions of the car</strong> (each has probability 1/3), and what happens in each:</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Car behind</th><th>Host opens</th><th>Stay with door 1</th><th>Switch</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Door 1</td><td>2 or 3</td><td>win</td><td>lose</td></tr><tr><td>Door 2</td><td>3 (must avoid the car)</td><td>lose</td><td>win</td></tr><tr><td>Door 3</td><td>2 (must avoid the car)</td><td>lose</td><td>win</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"2\">\n<li><strong>Count:</strong> staying wins in 1 of 3 cases; switching wins in 2 of 3.</li>\n<li><strong>Why intuition fails.</strong> It feels like 50/50 between two closed doors, but the host&#39;s choice is not random: he is forced to avoid the car. In two of the three worlds, he points you to it.</li>\n<li><strong>A sanity check with 100 doors.</strong> You pick one; the host opens 98 goat doors. Would you keep your 1-in-100 pick? Switching wins 99 times in 100.</li>\n<li><strong>The assumption.</strong> This works because the host always opens a goat door and always offers the switch. Say so in an interview.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> When a probability feels wrong, list the equally likely cases in a table. The table cannot be fooled.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-reasoning-behind-each-answer","text":"The reasoning behind each answer","level":3},{"id":"h-extra-puzzles-with-answers","text":"Extra puzzles with answers","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"qa","id":"5.2#1","src":"c","q":"Two dice rolled: P(sum = 7)?","a":"<p>6/36 = 1/6</p>\n","s":"Six of 36 equally likely pairs sum to 7: 1/6."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.2#2","src":"c","q":"Family has two children, at least one is a girl: P(both girls)?","a":"<p>1/3</p>\n","s":"List BB, BG, GB, GG; remove BB; one of the remaining three is GG: 1/3."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.2#3","src":"c","q":"Expected number of rolls of a fair die to get a 6?","a":"<p>6, geometric</p>\n","s":"Geometric with p = 1/6: expected 1/p = 6 rolls."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.2#4","src":"c","q":"Birthday problem: how many people make a shared birthday about 50% likely?","a":"<p>23 people give roughly a 50% chance that two share a birthday.</p>\n","s":"Because you compare all pairs: P(no match) drops below 50% at n = 23."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.2#5","src":"c","q":"Monty Hall: should you switch doors, and what is the win probability if you do?","a":"<p>Yes. Switching wins with probability 2/3 (staying wins only 1/3).</p>\n","s":"Switching wins whenever your first pick was wrong, which is 2/3 of the time."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.2#6","src":"c","q":"You flip a fair coin until first head. Expected flips?","a":"<p>2</p>\n","s":"Geometric with p = 1/2: expected 2 flips."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.2#7","src":"c","q":"Drawing 2 cards from a deck: P(both aces)?","a":"<p>4/52 x 3/51 = 1/221</p>\n","s":"(4/52) × (3/51) = 1/221."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.2#8","src":"c","q":"A biased coin with P(H)=0.7: how do you make a fair decision?","a":"<p>Flip twice: HT vs TH, discard HH/TT.</p>\n","s":"Flip twice: HT means heads, TH means tails, discard HH and TT and repeat."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.2#9","src":"c","q":"Expected value of a game: pay Rs 10, roll a die, win Rs 3 x face value. What is the expected value?","a":"<p>E = 3 x 3.5 = 10.5; fair-plus</p>\n","s":"Expected payout = 3 × 3.5 = Rs 10.5, so an expected profit of Rs 0.50 per game."},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-reasoning-behind-each-answer\">The reasoning behind each answer</h3>\n<p><strong>1. Two dice, sum 7.</strong> Out of 36 equally likely pairs, six sum to 7: (1,6), (2,5), (3,4), (4,3), (5,2), (6,1). Probability 6/36 = <strong>1/6</strong>, the most likely sum.</p>\n<p><strong>2. At least one girl.</strong> Equally likely families: BB, BG, GB, GG. &quot;At least one is a girl&quot; removes BB, leaving {BG, GB, GG}; only GG has two girls: <strong>1/3</strong>. (If the question were &quot;the <em>older</em> child is a girl&quot;, the answer would be 1/2: the wording changes the information.)</p>\n<p><strong>3. Rolls until the first 6.</strong> Geometric distribution with p = 1/6, expected value 1/p = <strong>6</strong>. Derivation: E = 1 + (5/6) E, so E/6 = 1 and E = 6.</p>\n<p><strong>4. The birthday problem.</strong> P(no shared birthday among n people) = (365/365)(364/365)…((365 − n + 1)/365). For n = 23 this is 0.493, so the chance of a shared birthday is <strong>50.7%</strong>. It feels too small because we compare all n(n − 1)/2 = 253 pairs, not just our own birthday. Run it:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"birthday","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<p><strong>5. Monty Hall.</strong> Your first pick is the car with probability 1/3. The host, who knows where the car is, always opens a goat door. If you were right at first (1/3), switching loses; if you were wrong (2/3), switching wins, because the host has revealed the only other goat. <strong>Switch: 2/3.</strong> Try it:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"montyhall","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<p><strong>6. Flips until the first head.</strong> Geometric with p = 1/2: expected <strong>2</strong> flips.</p>\n<p><strong>7. Two aces from the deck.</strong> 4/52 × 3/51 = 12/2652 = <strong>1/221</strong>. (Or C(4,2)/C(52,2) = 6/1326 = 1/221.)</p>\n<p><strong>8. Fair decision from a biased coin (von Neumann trick).</strong> Flip twice. HT and TH are equally likely (each p(1 − p)), whatever p is. Say heads for HT and tails for TH; on HH or TT discard and repeat. The decision is exactly fair.</p>\n<p><strong>9. The die game.</strong> Pay Rs 10, win Rs 3 × the face value. E[face] = 3.5, so the expected payout is 3 × 3.5 = Rs 10.5, an expected gain of <strong>Rs 0.50</strong> per play: fair-plus, a good bet in the long run.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-extra-puzzles-with-answers\">Extra puzzles with answers</h3>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-puzzles~1","q":"What is the expected number of coin flips to get two heads in a row (HH)?","a":"<p>Use states. Let E be the expected flips from the start, and H the expected flips when the last flip was a head. From the start: flip once; with probability 1/2 you are in state H (cost 1 flip + H), with probability 1/2 you stay at the start (1 flip + E). So E = 1 + ½H + ½E. From state H: flip again; with probability 1/2 you finish (1 flip), with probability 1/2 you fall back to the start (1 flip + E). So H = 1 + ½E. Substituting: E = 1 + ½(1 + ½E) + ½E = 1.5 + ¾E, so <strong>E = 6</strong>. (For HT the answer is 4, which is surprising: different patterns have different expected waiting times.)</p>\n","s":"6."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-puzzles~2","q":"Three cards: one is red on both sides, one is white on both sides, one is red on one side and white on the other. You draw one at random and see a red face. What is the probability the other side is also red?","a":"<p>There are six equally likely faces: R1, R2 (card 1), W1, W2 (card 2), R3, W3 (card 3). You saw a red face, which is R1, R2 or R3. For R1 and R2 the other side is red; for R3 it is white. So 2 of 3 equally likely red faces have a red back: <strong>2/3</strong>. The mistake is treating the two cards that <em>could</em> show red as equally likely; the double-red card has two ways to show red.</p>\n","s":"2/3, not 1/2."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-puzzles~3","q":"You roll a fair die up to four times hoping for a six (Chevalier de Méré&#39;s problem). Is it better than even odds?","a":"<p>P(at least one six in 4 rolls) = 1 − (5/6)⁴ = 1 − 0.482 = <strong>51.8%</strong>, slightly better than even. The famous companion bet (at least one double six in 24 rolls of two dice) is 1 − (35/36)²⁴ = 49.1%, slightly worse than even, which is how gamblers first discovered that intuition about small probabilities is unreliable.</p>\n","s":"Yes: 1 − (5/6)⁴ ≈ 51.8%."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-puzzles~ca0_1","q":"Monty Hall: why is switching better?","a":"<p>Your first pick wins 1/3. The host always reveals a goat from the other two, so the remaining closed door carries their combined 2/3.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-puzzles~ca0_2","q":"Why does the birthday paradox feel surprising?","a":"<p>We think of 23 people vs one date, but there are 253 pairs, each a chance of a match.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-puzzles~ca0_3","q":"Expected number of rolls of a fair die until the first six?","a":"<p>6 (geometric distribution, mean 1/p).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m5-puzzles~ta1_1","q":"<p>Five people are in a room. What is the probability that at least two share a birthday (ignore leap years)? Set it up with &quot;one minus none&quot;.</p>\n","a":"<p>P(all different) = (365/365) × (364/365) × (363/365) × (362/365) × (361/365) ≈ 0.973. So P(at least one shared birthday) ≈ 1 − 0.973 = 0.027, about 2.7%. With 23 people the same calculation passes 50%, because the number of pairs grows quickly: 23 people form 253 pairs.</p>\n"}]},"m5-distributions":{"id":"m5-distributions","slug":"distributions-you-must-know","m":5,"title":"Distributions you must know","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m5-probability"],"tags":["statistics","distributions"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"The distributions interviewers expect you to know: what each models, its parameters, mean and variance, typical use cases, and how to compute probabilities in Python.","keyTerms":["Bernoulli","binomial","Poisson","geometric","uniform","normal","exponential","t distribution","chi-square","F","68-95-99.7","z-score"],"interviewQs":["Which distribution models the number of orders per hour?","When does the binomial look like a normal?","What is the memoryless property of the exponential?"],"mistakes":["Using a normal for counts near zero","Confusing Poisson rate and exponential rate","Forgetting the continuity of continuous distributions (P(X = x) = 0)"],"practice":"For five business quantities (conversions, orders per minute, time between arrivals, heights, revenue), name the distribution and its parameters.","related":["m5-clt","m5-hypothesis","m5-probability"],"points":"Distribution - Type - Use case - Mean - Variance\nBernoulli(p) - Discrete - Single yes/no trial - p - p(1-p)\nBinomial(n,p) - Discrete - # successes in n trials (conversions out of visitors) - np - np(1-p)\nPoisson(lambda) - Discrete - Count of events in an interval (orders per hour) - lambda - lambda\nGeometric(p) - Discrete - Trials until first success - 1/p - (1-p)/p^2\nUniform(a,b) - Continuous - Equal likelihood - (a+b)/2 - (b-a)^2/12\nNormal(mu, sigma^2) - Continuous - Heights, errors, sums of many small effects - mu - sigma^2\nExponential(lambda) - Continuous - Time between Poisson events - 1/lambda - 1/lambda^2\nt-distribution - Continuous - Small-sample mean inference (unknown variance) - 0 - df/(df-2)\nChi-square - Continuous - Variance and categorical tests - k - 2k\nF - Continuous - Ratio of variances (ANOVA) - - - -\n\nNormal rule: 68-95-99.7 within 1, 2, 3 SDs. 95% interval uses z = 1.96."},"intuitive":"<p>A distribution is the &quot;shape&quot; of how likely each value is. Flip a coin once: Bernoulli. Count heads in 10 flips: binomial. Count how many customers walk in during one hour: Poisson. How long until the next customer: exponential. Heights and measurement errors pile up in a bell shape: normal. The t, chi-square and F shapes are the special ones statisticians use when they test things, and you only need to know where they appear.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Choosing the right distribution is the first step in modelling counts, waiting times and test statistics.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Match the story (yes/no, count of successes, count in an interval, waiting time, sum of many small effects) to the distribution.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A distribution says how likely each value is. Bernoulli: one yes or no trial. Binomial: number of successes in n independent trials. Poisson: count of events in a fixed interval when events are rare and independent, mean equal to variance. Normal: the bell curve, defined by mean and standard deviation, with about 68, 95 and 99.7 per cent within one, two and three standard deviations. Uniform: all values equally likely. Exponential: waiting time between Poisson events. Long-tailed ones like lognormal fit incomes and latencies. The catch: I pick a distribution from how the data is generated, such as counts or waiting times, and not just from how a histogram looks.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Turn business questions into distributions","html":"<p>A small café receives on average 3 online orders per hour, independently of each other.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Counts in a fixed time point to the Poisson.</strong> Orders per hour ~ Poisson(λ = 3), with mean 3 and variance 3.</li>\n<li><strong>P(no orders in an hour)</strong> = e^(−3) ≈ <strong>0.050</strong>. One hour in twenty is quiet.</li>\n<li><strong>P(at least one order)</strong> = 1 − 0.050 = <strong>0.95</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The full shape:</strong> P(0) ≈ 0.05, P(1) ≈ 0.15, P(2) ≈ 0.22, P(3) ≈ 0.22. The most likely counts are 2 and 3.</li>\n<li><strong>A different question, a different distribution.</strong> Of 10 visitors to the menu page, each orders with probability 20%. The number who order ~ Binomial(n = 10, p = 0.2), mean 2.</li>\n<li><strong>P(nobody orders)</strong> = 0.8^10 ≈ <strong>0.11</strong>. <strong>P(3 or more order)</strong> ≈ <strong>0.32</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Question shape</th><th>Distribution</th><th>Here</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Events per interval</td><td>Poisson(λ)</td><td>orders per hour, λ = 3</td></tr><tr><td>Successes out of n tries</td><td>Binomial(n, p)</td><td>buyers out of 10 visitors</td></tr><tr><td>Waiting time between events</td><td>Exponential</td><td>minutes until the next order</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> You choose a distribution from how the numbers are generated (counts in time, successes out of tries, waiting times), not from the shape of a histogram.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-catalogue-with-stories-and-python","text":"The catalogue, with stories and Python","level":3},{"id":"h-the-normal-distribution-and-the-68-95-99-7-rule","text":"The normal distribution and the 68–95–99.7 rule","level":3},{"id":"h-relationships-between-distributions","text":"Relationships between distributions","level":3},{"id":"h-continuous-versus-discrete","text":"Continuous versus discrete","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Distribution</th><th>Type</th><th>Use case</th><th>Mean</th><th>Variance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bernoulli(p)</td><td>Discrete</td><td>Single yes/no trial</td><td>p</td><td>p(1-p)</td></tr><tr><td>Binomial(n,p)</td><td>Discrete</td><td># successes in n trials (conversions out of visitors)</td><td>np</td><td>np(1-p)</td></tr><tr><td>Poisson(lambda)</td><td>Discrete</td><td>Count of events in an interval (orders per hour)</td><td>lambda</td><td>lambda</td></tr><tr><td>Geometric(p)</td><td>Discrete</td><td>Trials until first success</td><td>1/p</td><td>(1-p)/p^2</td></tr><tr><td>Uniform(a,b)</td><td>Continuous</td><td>Equal likelihood</td><td>(a+b)/2</td><td>(b-a)^2/12</td></tr><tr><td>Normal(mu, sigma^2)</td><td>Continuous</td><td>Heights, errors, sums of many small effects</td><td>mu</td><td>sigma^2</td></tr><tr><td>Exponential(lambda)</td><td>Continuous</td><td>Time between Poisson events</td><td>1/lambda</td><td>1/lambda^2</td></tr><tr><td>t-distribution</td><td>Continuous</td><td>Small-sample mean inference (unknown variance)</td><td>0</td><td>df/(df-2)</td></tr><tr><td>Chi-square</td><td>Continuous</td><td>Variance and categorical tests</td><td>k</td><td>2k</td></tr><tr><td>F</td><td>Continuous</td><td>Ratio of variances (ANOVA)</td><td>-</td><td>-</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Normal rule:</strong> 68-95-99.7 within 1, 2, 3 SDs. 95% interval uses z = 1.96.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-catalogue-with-stories-and-python\">The catalogue, with stories and Python</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Distribution</th><th>Story</th><th>Parameters</th><th>Mean</th><th>Variance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Bernoulli(p)</td><td>one yes/no trial</td><td>p</td><td>p</td><td>p(1−p)</td></tr><tr><td>Binomial(n, p)</td><td>successes in n independent trials</td><td>n, p</td><td>np</td><td>np(1−p)</td></tr><tr><td>Poisson(λ)</td><td>events in a fixed interval</td><td>λ</td><td>λ</td><td>λ</td></tr><tr><td>Geometric(p)</td><td>trials until first success</td><td>p</td><td>1/p</td><td>(1−p)/p²</td></tr><tr><td>Uniform(a, b)</td><td>all values equally likely</td><td>a, b</td><td>(a+b)/2</td><td>(b−a)²/12</td></tr><tr><td>Normal(μ, σ²)</td><td>sum of many small effects</td><td>μ, σ</td><td>μ</td><td>σ²</td></tr><tr><td>Exponential(λ)</td><td>waiting time between Poisson events</td><td>λ</td><td>1/λ</td><td>1/λ²</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> scipy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> stats\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Conversions: 10 visitors, each converts with p = 0.1. P(at least 2 convert)?</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span> - stats.binom.cdf(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, n=<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>, p=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.1</span>)          <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 0.264  (mean 1, variance 0.9)</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Orders: average 3 per hour. P(exactly 5 in an hour)? P(more than 5)?</span>\nstats.poisson.pmf(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, mu=<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>)                    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 0.1008</span>\nstats.poisson.sf(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>, mu=<span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>)                     <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 0.0839</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Waiting time: events arrive at rate 0.5 per minute (mean wait 2 min). P(wait &gt; 2 minutes)?</span>\nstats.expon.sf(<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, scale=<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>)                    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 0.368 = e^-1</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Normal: P(Z &lt; 1.96) and the 68-95-99.7 rule</span>\nstats.norm.cdf(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1.96</span>)                          <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 0.975</span>\nstats.norm.cdf(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>) - stats.norm.cdf(-<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 0.683</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-normal-distribution-and-the-68-95-99-7-rule\">The normal distribution and the 68–95–99.7 rule</h3>\n<p>About <strong>68%</strong> of values fall within 1 SD of the mean, <strong>95%</strong> within 2 SDs (more precisely 1.96), <strong>99.7%</strong> within 3. Standardising with <code>z = (x − μ)/σ</code> turns any normal into the standard normal, so one table serves all. Explore it:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"normal","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-relationships-between-distributions\">Relationships between distributions</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bernoulli → binomial:</strong> a binomial is a sum of n independent Bernoullis.</li>\n<li><strong>Binomial → normal:</strong> for large n (when np and n(1−p) are both above about 10) the binomial is approximately normal, which underlies the z-test for proportions.</li>\n<li><strong>Binomial → Poisson:</strong> many trials, tiny p, moderate np = λ.</li>\n<li><strong>Poisson ↔ exponential:</strong> if events arrive at rate λ per unit time (Poisson counts), the waiting time between events is exponential with mean 1/λ. The exponential is <strong>memoryless</strong>: given you have already waited 10 minutes, the remaining wait has the same distribution as a fresh wait.</li>\n<li><strong>Normal → t, chi-square, F:</strong> the <strong>t</strong> distribution looks like a normal with heavier tails and appears when you estimate σ from a small sample (it converges to the normal as degrees of freedom grow: the 97.5th percentile is 2.23 at 10 df, 1.98 at 100 df, 1.96 at infinity). <strong>Chi-square</strong> is a sum of squared standard normals (variances, categorical tests). <strong>F</strong> is a ratio of chi-squares (ANOVA, comparing variances).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-continuous-versus-discrete\">Continuous versus discrete</h3>\n<p>For a continuous variable, P(X = exact value) = 0; probabilities come from areas under the density (intervals). For discrete variables the probability mass function gives probabilities at points.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-distributions~1","q":"Which distribution would you use for: (a) number of support tickets per day, (b) time until the next ticket arrives, (c) whether a single email is opened, (d) number of opened emails out of 500 sent?","a":"<p>(a) Tickets per day are counts in a fixed interval: <strong>Poisson(λ = average per day)</strong>. (b) The waiting time until the next ticket is <strong>exponential</strong> (mean 1/λ), memoryless. (c) One yes/no outcome: <strong>Bernoulli(p)</strong>. (d) Number of successes in n = 500 independent trials: <strong>binomial(500, p)</strong>, approximately normal for large n. State the assumptions (independence, constant rate) and mention overdispersion if real counts vary more than a Poisson predicts.</p>\n","s":"(a) Poisson, (b) exponential, (c) Bernoulli, (d) binomial."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-distributions~ca0_1","q":"Which distribution for the number of support tickets per hour?","a":"<p>Poisson (counts of independent events in an interval), check for overdispersion.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-distributions~ca0_2","q":"Heights are N(170, 10). What share lies between 160 and 180?","a":"<p>About 68% (within one standard deviation).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-distributions~ca0_3","q":"Binomial versus Poisson: when does one approximate the other?","a":"<p>Binomial with large n and small p approximates Poisson with λ = np.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m5-distributions~ta1_1","q":"<p>Orders arrive on average every 20 minutes (a Poisson process). What is the probability that you wait more than 30 minutes for the next one? Name the distribution you use.</p>\n","a":"<p>The waiting time between Poisson events is exponential with rate λ = 1/20 per minute. P(wait &gt; t) = e^(−λt) = e^(−30/20) = e^(−1.5) ≈ 0.22. So about 22% of the time you wait more than half an hour. The exponential is memoryless: having already waited 10 minutes does not shorten the remaining wait.</p>\n"}]},"m5-clt":{"id":"m5-clt","slug":"central-limit-theorem-and-the-law-of-large-numbers","m":5,"title":"Central Limit Theorem and the Law of Large Numbers","min":25,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m5-distributions"],"tags":["statistics","inference","simulation"],"qa":6,"ai":{"scope":"The Central Limit Theorem and Law of Large Numbers, standard error, when the CLT applies and fails, and how to see it by simulation.","keyTerms":["central limit theorem","standard error","law of large numbers","sampling distribution","bootstrap","heavy tails"],"interviewQs":["Explain the CLT to a non-technical person.","How does the CLT differ from the Law of Large Numbers?","When does the CLT not apply?"],"mistakes":["Saying the data becomes normal (it is the sample mean that does)","Applying rule-of-thumb n >= 30 to heavy-tailed data","Confusing standard deviation with standard error"],"practice":"Simulate sample means from an exponential distribution for n = 1, 5, 30, 200 and watch the shape change.","related":["m5-distributions","m5-hypothesis","m6-lifecycle"],"points":"For i.i.d. samples with finite variance, the distribution of the sample mean approaches Normal(mu, sigma^2/n) as n grows, regardless of the population's shape. Standard error = sigma / sqrt(n). This is why we can run z/t-tests on non-normal data when n is large enough (rule of thumb n >= 30, more for heavy skew).\n\nLaw of Large Numbers: the sample mean converges to the true mean as n increases. (CLT is about the shape of the distribution; LLN about convergence.)"},"intuitive":"<p>Imagine a huge jar of jellybeans of every size. Grab a handful, work out the average size, and write it down. Do it again and again. Even if the jar is messy, your list of averages forms a neat bell shape, and it gets tighter the more beans you grab each time. The bell is centred on the true average size of all beans. This is why we can use a small sample to learn about a whole population, and why so many tests rely on the bell curve even when the original data is lopsided.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> It is the reason z- and t-tests and confidence intervals work on non-normal data, including A/B tests on revenue.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Averages of big enough samples behave nicely (bell-shaped), with a spread that shrinks like 1/√n.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;The Central Limit Theorem says that the average of many independent samples is approximately normal, whatever the shape of the original data, provided the variance is finite. The spread of that average, the standard error, is sigma over root n, so quadrupling the sample halves the error. This is why we can build confidence intervals and t-tests for means even on skewed data. The Law of Large Numbers is the companion: as n grows, the sample mean converges to the true mean. The catch: CLT is about means, not about the raw data becoming normal, and it needs a large enough n, more so for heavy skew.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Watch averages become predictable","html":"<p>One fair die: the values 1 to 6 are equally likely (a flat distribution, nothing like a bell). Mean 3.5, standard deviation about 1.71.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>One roll</strong> can land anywhere from 1 to 6 with equal chance.</li>\n<li><strong>The average of 4 rolls.</strong> It is still centred on 3.5, but its spread shrinks to 1.71 / √4 ≈ <strong>0.85</strong>. Averages of 1 or 6 are now rare, because all four rolls would have to be extreme.</li>\n<li><strong>The average of 100 rolls.</strong> Spread (standard error) = 1.71 / √100 ≈ <strong>0.17</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The shape is now a bell.</strong> By the Central Limit Theorem, the 100-roll average is approximately normal, so about 95% of such averages land within 3.5 ± 1.96 × 0.17, that is between about <strong>3.17 and 3.83</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>The cost of precision.</strong> To halve the standard error you need four times the rolls: 400 rolls give 1.71 / 20 ≈ 0.085.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Rolls averaged</th><th>Standard error</th><th>95% of averages fall within</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>1.71</td><td>(no bell shape yet)</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.85</td><td>roughly 1.8 to 5.2</td></tr><tr><td>100</td><td>0.17</td><td>3.17 to 3.83</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Individual values can be wild, but averages are predictable, and their spread shrinks with √n. That is why a sample can tell us about a population.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-statement-precisely","text":"The statement, precisely","level":3},{"id":"h-see-it-happen","text":"See it happen","level":3},{"id":"h-when-the-clt-is-a-poor-guide","text":"When the CLT is a poor guide","level":3},{"id":"h-why-it-matters-for-a-b-tests","text":"Why it matters for A/B tests","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>For i.i.d. samples with finite variance, the distribution of the sample mean approaches Normal(mu, sigma^2/n) as n grows, regardless of the population&#39;s shape. <strong>Standard error = sigma / sqrt(n).</strong> This is why we can run z/t-tests on non-normal data when n is large enough (rule of thumb n &gt;= 30, more for heavy skew).</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Law of Large Numbers:</strong> the sample mean converges to the true mean as n increases. (CLT is about the <em>shape</em> of the distribution; LLN about <em>convergence</em>.)</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-statement-precisely\">The statement, precisely</h3>\n<p>If X₁, …, Xₙ are independent, identically distributed with mean μ and finite variance σ², then the sample mean X̄ satisfies, for large n:</p>\n<div class=\"math-block\"><span class=\"katex\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><semantics><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>X</mi><mo>ˉ</mo></mover><mtext>  </mtext><mo>≈</mo><mtext>  </mtext><mtext>Normal</mtext><mtext> ⁣</mtext><mrow><mo fence=\"true\">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mtext> </mtext><mfrac><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mspace width=\"2em\"/><mtext>standard error</mtext><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>σ</mi><msqrt><mi>n</mi></msqrt></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">.</mi></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar X \\;\\approx\\; \\text{Normal}\\!\\left(\\mu,\\ \\frac{\\sigma^2}{n}\\right), \\qquad \\text{standard error} = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\sqrt n}.</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li>It is the <strong>distribution of the sample mean</strong> that becomes normal, not the data.</li>\n<li>The <strong>standard error (SE)</strong> measures how much sample means vary from sample to sample. The <strong>standard deviation (SD)</strong> measures how much individual values vary. To halve the SE you need <strong>four times</strong> the data.</li>\n<li><strong>Law of Large Numbers (LLN):</strong> as n grows, X̄ converges to μ (it says <em>where</em> the mean goes). The CLT describes the <em>shape</em> of the fluctuations around it.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-see-it-happen\">See it happen</h3>\n<p>Pick a very skewed population and increase the sample size:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"clt","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<p>The same in code (exponential population with mean 1, so σ = 1):</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> scipy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> stats\nrng = np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)\n\npop = rng.exponential(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">10_000</span>)\nstats.skew(pop)                                      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># about 2.0  very right-skewed</span>\n\nmeans = rng.exponential(<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, (<span class=\"hljs-number\">10_000</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">30</span>)).mean(axis=<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 10,000 sample means, each from n = 30</span>\nstats.skew(means)                                    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># about 0.4  much closer to symmetric</span>\nmeans.std()                                          <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># about 0.184  ~ sigma / sqrt(30) = 0.183</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-when-the-clt-is-a-poor-guide\">When the CLT is a poor guide</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Small n with strong skew or outliers.</strong> The n ≥ 30 rule of thumb is a <em>minimum for mild skew</em>; for heavy skew (revenue per user, where a few whales dominate) you may need thousands of observations. Use the bootstrap or transform (log) the data.</li>\n<li><strong>Heavy tails without finite variance</strong> (for example a Cauchy distribution, or power-law data): the CLT does not apply and the sample mean does not stabilise.</li>\n<li><strong>Dependent data</strong> (time series, users seen repeatedly in sessions): the effective sample size is smaller than n, so SEs are understated. Aggregate to the independent unit (the user) first.</li>\n<li><strong>Very small proportions</strong> with few events: the normal approximation to the binomial fails; use exact methods.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"h-why-it-matters-for-a-b-tests\">Why it matters for A/B tests</h3>\n<p>For a conversion-rate test, each visitor is a Bernoulli trial; the difference in observed conversion rates is approximately normal with SE = √(p(1−p)(1/n₁ + 1/n₂)), which is what the z-test uses. For revenue per user the same logic holds for the mean, but check skew and outliers before trusting it.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-clt~1","q":"A survey of 100 people gives a mean satisfaction of 7.2 with standard deviation 1.5. What is the standard error, and roughly what is the 95% confidence interval for the true mean?","a":"<p>SE = σ / √n = 1.5 / 10 = <strong>0.15</strong>. A 95% confidence interval is about mean ± 1.96 × SE = 7.2 ± 0.29 = <strong>(6.91, 7.49)</strong>. With n = 100 the t critical value is about 1.98, which gives nearly the same interval. To narrow the interval to ±0.15 you would need four times the sample size (n = 400).</p>\n","s":"SE = 1.5/√100 = 0.15; CI ≈ 7.2 ± 1.96 × 0.15 = (6.91, 7.49)."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-clt~2","q":"What is the difference between the standard deviation and the standard error?","a":"<p>The <strong>standard deviation</strong> describes how far individual observations are from the mean; it does not shrink as you collect more data. The <strong>standard error</strong> of the mean, σ/√n, describes how far the <em>sample mean</em> is from the true mean; it shrinks as n grows. Error bars on a chart should say which one they show.</p>\n","s":"SD describes the spread of the data; SE describes the spread of a statistic (like the mean) across repeated samples."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-clt~ca0_1","q":"Std dev of a metric is 20, n = 100. Standard error of the mean?","a":"<p>20 / 10 = 2.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-clt~ca0_2","q":"How much more data to halve the standard error?","a":"<p>Four times as much, since error scales with 1/√n.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-clt~ca0_3","q":"LLN versus CLT in one line each?","a":"<p>LLN: the sample mean approaches the true mean. CLT: the sample mean&#39;s distribution around it is approximately normal with SD σ/√n.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m5-clt~ta1_1","q":"<p>Delivery times have a standard deviation of 12 minutes (and a skewed shape). You average 36 deliveries. What is the standard error of the average, and within what range around the true mean do about 95% of such averages fall?</p>\n","a":"<p>Standard error = 12 / √36 = 2 minutes. By the CLT the average of 36 is approximately normal even though single deliveries are skewed, so about 95% of averages fall within the true mean ± 1.96 × 2 ≈ ± 3.9 minutes. To get ± 2 minutes you need four times the data: 144 deliveries.</p>\n"}]},"m5-hypothesis":{"id":"m5-hypothesis","slug":"hypothesis-testing-the-core-definitions","m":5,"title":"Hypothesis testing: the core definitions","min":35,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m5-clt"],"tags":["statistics","inference","hypothesis-testing"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"The logic of hypothesis testing: null and alternative hypotheses, p-values and what they do not mean, errors, power, confidence intervals and effect size, with a worked t-test.","keyTerms":["null hypothesis","p-value","significance level","Type I error","Type II error","power","confidence interval","effect size","Cohen's d"],"interviewQs":["Explain a p-value to a non-technical person.","What is the difference between Type I and Type II errors?","Does a 95% confidence interval mean there is a 95% probability the parameter is inside it?","Why can a result be statistically significant but unimportant?"],"mistakes":["Interpreting the p-value as the probability that H0 is true","Saying \"we accept the null\" after a non-significant result","Reporting a p-value without an effect size or interval"],"practice":"Take a two-group dataset, run a Welch t-test, and write the interpretation in three sentences: effect size with interval, p-value meaning, practical significance.","related":["m5-which-test","m5-clt","m6-lifecycle"],"points":"- Null hypothesis (H0): default, no effect. Alternative (H1): what we want evidence for.\n- p-value: probability of observing data at least as extreme as ours, assuming H0 is true. It is NOT the probability that H0 is true, and NOT the probability the result is due to chance.\n- Significance level alpha: tolerated Type I error rate (usually 0.05).\n- Type I error (false positive): reject H0 when true; probability alpha.\n- Type II error (false negative): fail to reject H0 when false; probability beta.\n- Power = 1 - beta: probability of detecting a true effect (target 80%).\n- Confidence interval (95%): if we repeated the procedure many times, about 95% of such intervals would contain the true parameter. It is NOT \"95% probability the parameter lies in this particular interval\" (frequentist reading).\n- Effect size: magnitude (Cohen's d, difference in proportions, odds ratio). Statistical significance does not imply practical significance.\n\n\"If our new feature actually did nothing, how surprising would it be to see a result this big just from random variation? A p-value of 0.03 means that would happen only about 3% of the time, so we have reasonable evidence something real is going on.\""},"intuitive":"<p>A hypothesis test is a courtroom for an idea. We start by assuming the boring explanation is true (&quot;the new button changed nothing&quot;, the null hypothesis). Then we look at the data and ask: if the boring explanation were true, how surprising would results this extreme be? If they would be very surprising (a small p-value, say below 5%), we stop believing the boring explanation. A small p-value does not say the new idea is definitely right; it says the data is hard to explain by luck alone. And a big p-value does not prove nothing is happening; it may just mean we did not collect enough evidence.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Every A/B test and most analytical claims rely on this logic, and interviewers love testing the misconceptions.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> p-value = how surprising the data is if nothing is going on. Report the size of the effect too.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Hypothesis testing asks whether data is surprising under a default assumption. The null says there is no effect; the alternative says there is. I pick a significance level, usually five per cent. The p-value is the probability of data at least this extreme if the null were true. A small p-value means the data is hard to explain by chance, so we reject the null. Type one error is a false alarm, with probability alpha. Type two is a miss, and power is one minus its probability. A confidence interval gives the plausible range for the effect. The catch: the p-value is not the probability that the null is true, and statistical significance does not mean practical importance.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Build a p-value from a coin","html":"<p>A friend&#39;s coin lands heads 15 times in 20 flips. Is it fair?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Assume the boring explanation (the null hypothesis).</strong> The coin is fair: P(heads) = 0.5.</li>\n<li><strong>Ask how surprising the data is under that assumption.</strong> P(15 or more heads in 20 flips | fair) = sum of the binomial probabilities for 15, 16, ..., 20 ≈ <strong>0.021</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Count both directions.</strong> 15 tails would be just as suspicious, so the two-sided p-value doubles it: ≈ <strong>0.041</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Compare with your threshold.</strong> Using α = 0.05, 0.041 &lt; 0.05, so you reject &quot;fair&quot;. Data this lopsided would happen about 4 times in 100 if the coin were fair.</li>\n<li><strong>Say what it is not.</strong> It is not &quot;a 4% chance the coin is fair&quot;. It is the chance of results this extreme <em>if</em> it were fair.</li>\n<li><strong>Remember the two ways to be wrong.</strong> If the coin really is fair, you have made a Type I error (a false alarm), which happens 5% of the time with α = 0.05. With only 20 flips, a slightly biased coin (say 55% heads) would usually go undetected: a Type II error, caused by low power.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Every hypothesis test has this shape: assume no effect, compute how surprising the data would be, and reject the assumption if it is surprising enough.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"c-5-4-key-definitions","text":"Key definitions","level":2},{"id":"c-5-4-explain-p-value-to-a-non-technical-person","text":"Explain p-value to a non-technical person","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-vocabulary-with-what-each-thing-means","text":"The vocabulary, with what each thing means","level":3},{"id":"h-a-worked-example-welch-t-test","text":"A worked example: Welch t-test","level":3},{"id":"h-the-misconceptions-interviewers-test","text":"The misconceptions interviewers test","level":3},{"id":"h-explaining-the-p-value-to-a-non-technical-person","text":"Explaining the p-value to a non-technical person","level":3},{"id":"h-confidence-intervals-and-tests-agree","text":"Confidence intervals and tests agree","level":3},{"id":"h-one-sided-versus-two-sided","text":"One-sided versus two-sided","level":3},{"id":"h-what-affects-power","text":"What affects power","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-5-4-key-definitions\">Key definitions</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Null hypothesis (H0):</strong> default, no effect. <strong>Alternative (H1):</strong> what we want evidence for.</li>\n<li><strong>p-value:</strong> probability of observing data at least as extreme as ours, assuming H0 is true. It is NOT the probability that H0 is true, and NOT the probability the result is due to chance.</li>\n<li><strong>Significance level alpha:</strong> tolerated Type I error rate (usually 0.05).</li>\n<li><strong>Type I error (false positive):</strong> reject H0 when true; probability alpha.</li>\n<li><strong>Type II error (false negative):</strong> fail to reject H0 when false; probability beta.</li>\n<li><strong>Power = 1 - beta:</strong> probability of detecting a true effect (target 80%).</li>\n<li><strong>Confidence interval (95%):</strong> if we repeated the procedure many times, about 95% of such intervals would contain the true parameter. It is NOT &quot;95% probability the parameter lies in this particular interval&quot; (frequentist reading).</li>\n<li><strong>Effect size:</strong> magnitude (Cohen&#39;s d, difference in proportions, odds ratio). Statistical significance does not imply practical significance.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-5-4-explain-p-value-to-a-non-technical-person\">Explain p-value to a non-technical person</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>&quot;If our new feature actually did nothing, how surprising would it be to see a result this big just from random variation? A p-value of 0.03 means that would happen only about 3% of the time, so we have reasonable evidence something real is going on.&quot;</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-vocabulary-with-what-each-thing-means\">The vocabulary, with what each thing means</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Null hypothesis H₀</strong>: the default, &quot;no effect&quot;. <strong>Alternative H₁</strong>: what we hope to find evidence for (two-sided: &quot;different&quot;; one-sided: &quot;greater&quot;).</li>\n<li><strong>Test statistic</strong>: a number summarising how far the data is from H₀ in standard-error units (for example t = (difference in means) / SE).</li>\n<li><strong>p-value</strong>: the probability, <em>assuming H₀ is true</em>, of seeing a test statistic at least as extreme as the one observed.</li>\n<li><strong>Significance level α</strong> (commonly 0.05): the false-positive rate you are willing to accept. Reject H₀ if p &lt; α.</li>\n<li><strong>Type I error</strong> (false positive): reject a true H₀; probability α. <strong>Type II error</strong> (false negative): fail to reject a false H₀; probability β. <strong>Power = 1 − β</strong> (aim for 80% or more): the chance of detecting a real effect of a given size.</li>\n<li><strong>Effect size</strong>: how large the difference is (difference in means or proportions, Cohen&#39;s d = difference / pooled SD, odds ratio). <strong>Confidence interval (CI)</strong>: a range of plausible values for the effect.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>See how α, effect size and sample size trade off. The two curves are the distribution of the test statistic when H₀ is true and when the effect is real; the shaded areas are the error rates:</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"hypothesis","props":{}},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-a-worked-example-welch-t-test\">A worked example: Welch t-test</h3>\n<p>Delivery times (minutes) for 8 orders under the old routing (A) and 8 under the new routing (B):</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> scipy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> stats\na = np.array([<span class=\"hljs-number\">12.1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">11.8</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">13.4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">12.9</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">12.2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">13.0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">12.6</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">11.9</span>])      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># mean 12.49, sd 0.58</span>\nb = np.array([<span class=\"hljs-number\">13.5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">14.1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">13.2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">14.8</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">13.9</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">14.4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">13.7</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">14.0</span>])      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># mean 13.95, sd 0.50</span>\nt, p = stats.ttest_ind(a, b, equal_var=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>)                        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Welch: does not assume equal variances</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">round</span>(t, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>), p)                                                <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># -5.4  9.97e-05</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>Read it like this: the difference in means is 1.46 minutes; the Welch t-statistic is −5.4 (the difference is 5.4 standard errors); the p-value is about 0.0001, so data this extreme would occur about 1 time in 10,000 if the two routings were truly identical. Cohen&#39;s d is about 2.7, a very large effect, which is unrealistic for real delivery data (this is a toy example).</p>\n<p>The conclusion to write: <em>&quot;The new routing is slower by about 1.5 minutes (95% CI roughly 0.9 to 2.0; p &lt; 0.001).&quot;</em> Always give the direction, the size and the uncertainty, not only &quot;significant&quot;.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-misconceptions-interviewers-test\">The misconceptions interviewers test</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Why it is wrong</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>&quot;p = 0.03 means a 3% chance H₀ is true.&quot;</td><td>The p-value is computed <em>assuming</em> H₀; it is P(data</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;p = 0.03 means the result is 97% likely to be real / not due to chance.&quot;</td><td>Same error. The real probability depends on the prior plausibility and the power.</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;p &gt; 0.05 proves there is no effect.&quot;</td><td>It means insufficient evidence against H₀; an underpowered test often misses real effects.</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;A 95% CI contains the true value with 95% probability.&quot;</td><td>In the frequentist view the true value is fixed; the <em>procedure</em> captures it in 95% of repeated samples. Informally people use the Bayesian reading.</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;Significant means important.&quot;</td><td>With huge n, tiny, useless effects become significant. Look at effect size and cost.</td></tr><tr><td>&quot;p &lt; 0.05 therefore replicable.&quot;</td><td>A single test at α = 0.05 will be a false positive 5% of the time when H₀ is true, and more often if many were tried.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-explaining-the-p-value-to-a-non-technical-person\">Explaining the p-value to a non-technical person</h3>\n<p>&quot;If our new feature actually did nothing, how surprising would it be to see a result this big just from random variation? A p-value of 0.03 means that would happen only about 3% of the time, so we have reasonable evidence something real is going on.&quot;</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-confidence-intervals-and-tests-agree\">Confidence intervals and tests agree</h3>\n<p>A 95% CI for a difference that excludes 0 corresponds to p &lt; 0.05 for H₀: difference = 0. Intervals are more informative because they show the range of plausible effect sizes, which lets you judge practical importance.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-one-sided-versus-two-sided\">One-sided versus two-sided</h3>\n<p>Two-sided tests detect an effect in either direction and are the default. One-sided tests have more power in one direction but are justified only if the other direction is truly irrelevant and declared <em>before</em> seeing data.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-what-affects-power\">What affects power</h3>\n<p>Power increases with a larger <strong>effect size</strong>, a larger <strong>sample size</strong>, a larger <strong>α</strong>, and lower <strong>variance</strong> (and with a one-sided test in the right direction). Planning sample size in advance is the subject of the A/B testing module.</p>\n<p>Coverage of confidence intervals: run many experiments and see how often the interval captures the truth.</p>\n"},{"k":"w","src":"a","name":"ci","props":{}},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-hypothesis~1","q":"A test of a new feature returns p = 0.048, and a colleague tests 20 other metrics on the same experiment and finds one with p = 0.03. Which result do you trust more, and why?","a":"<p>When you test many metrics, the chance of at least one false positive rises (with 20 independent tests at α = 0.05 it is 1 − 0.95²⁰ ≈ 64%). A result found by searching through metrics is <strong>exploratory</strong>; a <strong>pre-specified primary metric</strong> is <strong>confirmatory</strong>. Trust the primary metric, treat the other finding as a hypothesis to test in a new experiment, or apply a multiple-comparison correction (Bonferroni, Holm or Benjamini-Hochberg). Also check effect size and whether p = 0.048 is borderline enough that a small analysis choice would change it.</p>\n","s":"Neither blindly: with 21 looks, about one false positive is expected; the pre-registered primary metric deserves more trust."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-hypothesis~ca0_1","q":"Explain p = 0.03 in one sentence to a product manager.","a":"<p>If there were truly no difference, we would see a gap this large or larger only about 3% of the time.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-hypothesis~ca0_2","q":"What is power and how do you raise it?","a":"<p>Probability of detecting a real effect: raise it with more samples, a larger effect, lower variance, or a higher alpha.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-hypothesis~ca0_3","q":"Is p = 0.03 evidence the null is 3% likely to be true?","a":"<p>No. It is P(data | null), not P(null | data); that needs a prior (Bayes).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m5-hypothesis~ta1_1","q":"<p>A coin lands heads 60 times in 100 flips. Using the normal approximation (under the null the number of heads has mean 50 and standard deviation √(100 × 0.5 × 0.5) = 5), compute z and the approximate two-sided p-value. Is it significant at 5%?</p>\n","a":"<p>z = (60 − 50) / 5 = 2. The two-sided p-value for z = 2 is about 0.046 (since 95% of a normal lies within ± 1.96). That is just under 0.05, so it is borderline significant. The exact binomial calculation gives about 0.057, just over 0.05. The lesson: a result this close to the threshold is weak evidence either way, so report the estimate (60%) with its interval rather than a verdict.</p>\n"}]},"m5-which-test":{"id":"m5-which-test","slug":"which-test-do-i-use","m":5,"title":"Which test do I use?","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m5-hypothesis"],"tags":["statistics","hypothesis-testing"],"qa":10,"ai":{"scope":"A decision guide for choosing the right statistical test from the type of data and the design, with assumptions and worked examples for each.","keyTerms":["z-test","t-test","Welch","paired t-test","ANOVA","chi-square","Mann-Whitney","Wilcoxon","Kruskal-Wallis","Shapiro-Wilk","Levene"],"interviewQs":["When do you use a paired t-test instead of an independent t-test?","Two conversion rates: which test and why?","What do you do if the data is not normal?"],"mistakes":["Using an independent test on paired data","Running multiple t-tests instead of ANOVA","Testing normality with a huge n and overreacting to tiny deviations"],"practice":"Work through the scenario questions below without looking at the answers, then check and note which assumption you missed.","related":["m5-hypothesis","m5-multiple-testing","m6-lifecycle"],"points":"Scenario - Test\nMean of one sample vs known value, sigma known, large n - One-sample z-test\nMean vs known value, sigma unknown - One-sample t-test\nCompare means of two independent groups - Two-sample (Welch's) t-test\nBefore/after on the same subjects - Paired t-test\nCompare means of 3+ groups - One-way ANOVA (then post-hoc Tukey)\nCompare two proportions (conversion rates) - Two-proportion z-test or chi-square\nAssociation between two categorical variables - Chi-square test of independence\nGoodness of fit to a distribution - Chi-square GoF, Kolmogorov-Smirnov\nNon-normal, small samples, two groups - Mann-Whitney U (independent), Wilcoxon signed-rank (paired)\nNon-normal, 3+ groups - Kruskal-Wallis\nTest normality - Shapiro-Wilk, QQ plot\nEquality of variances - Levene's test, F-test\nCorrelation significance - Pearson/Spearman test\n\nAssumptions to know for t-test: independence, approximate normality of the mean (or large n), (for Student's version) equal variance. Prefer Welch's."},"intuitive":"<p>Choosing a test is like choosing a tool from a toolbox by asking three questions. What kind of data do I have (numbers like time, or categories like yes/no)? How many groups am I comparing (one, two, or more)? Are the groups separate people or the same people measured twice? Answer those and the right test appears. If the numbers are very lopsided and the sample is small, switch to the &quot;rank-based&quot; cousin of the test that does not need a bell curve.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> &quot;Which test would you use here?&quot; is among the most common stats questions.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Type of outcome, number of groups, paired or independent, and whether normality is reasonable.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;Choosing a test comes down to three questions. What type is the outcome? How many groups, and are they independent or paired? Are assumptions met? Comparing means of two independent groups: t-test, or Welch&#39;s if variances differ. Paired before and after: paired t-test. Three or more groups: ANOVA. Comparing proportions: z-test or chi-square. Two categorical variables: chi-square test of independence. Non-normal or ordinal data with small samples: Mann-Whitney or Wilcoxon. Correlation: Pearson or Spearman. The catch: with a large n the CLT makes the t-test fine on skewed data, but heavy-tailed metrics like revenue may still need a bootstrap or a transform.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Choose the test with three questions","html":"<p>For each scenario ask: (1) Is the outcome a number or a category? (2) How many groups? (3) Are the groups independent or paired?</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>&quot;Did the new checkout raise the conversion rate?&quot;</strong> Outcome: converted yes or no (a category, summarised as a proportion). Two independent groups. <strong>Two-proportion z-test</strong> (or chi-square).</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Is average delivery time different between two cities?&quot;</strong> A number, two independent groups. <strong>Welch&#39;s t-test.</strong> Delivery times are skewed, but with hundreds of orders per city the CLT protects the test of means.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Did a training course raise the same employees&#39; scores?&quot;</strong> A number, the same people measured twice: <strong>paired t-test</strong> on the differences.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Do three ad designs differ in average time on page?&quot;</strong> A number, three groups: <strong>ANOVA</strong>, then pairwise comparisons with a correction.</li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Is device type related to whether a user churns?&quot;</strong> Two categorical variables: <strong>chi-square test of independence.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Eight customers rated two menus on a 1-to-5 scale.&quot;</strong> Small, ordinal and paired: <strong>Wilcoxon signed-rank test.</strong></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> You do not memorise a list of tests. Three questions about the data lead to the test, and saying them aloud is what earns the marks.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-decision-guide","text":"The decision guide","level":3},{"id":"h-worked-examples-with-python","text":"Worked examples with Python","level":3},{"id":"h-scenario-drill","text":"Scenario drill","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Scenario</th><th>Test</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mean of one sample vs known value, sigma known, large n</td><td>One-sample z-test</td></tr><tr><td>Mean vs known value, sigma unknown</td><td>One-sample t-test</td></tr><tr><td>Compare means of two independent groups</td><td>Two-sample (Welch&#39;s) t-test</td></tr><tr><td>Before/after on the same subjects</td><td>Paired t-test</td></tr><tr><td>Compare means of 3+ groups</td><td>One-way ANOVA (then post-hoc Tukey)</td></tr><tr><td>Compare two proportions (conversion rates)</td><td>Two-proportion z-test or chi-square</td></tr><tr><td>Association between two categorical variables</td><td>Chi-square test of independence</td></tr><tr><td>Goodness of fit to a distribution</td><td>Chi-square GoF, Kolmogorov-Smirnov</td></tr><tr><td>Non-normal, small samples, two groups</td><td>Mann-Whitney U (independent), Wilcoxon signed-rank (paired)</td></tr><tr><td>Non-normal, 3+ groups</td><td>Kruskal-Wallis</td></tr><tr><td>Test normality</td><td>Shapiro-Wilk, QQ plot</td></tr><tr><td>Equality of variances</td><td>Levene&#39;s test, F-test</td></tr><tr><td>Correlation significance</td><td>Pearson/Spearman test</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p><strong>Assumptions to know for t-test:</strong> independence, approximate normality of the mean (or large n), (for Student&#39;s version) equal variance. Prefer Welch&#39;s.</p>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-decision-guide\">The decision guide</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Outcome</th><th>Groups / design</th><th>Usual test</th><th>If assumptions fail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Numeric mean vs a known value</td><td>one sample</td><td>one-sample t-test (z if σ known, large n)</td><td>Wilcoxon signed-rank</td></tr><tr><td>Numeric</td><td>two independent groups</td><td><strong>Welch&#39;s t-test</strong></td><td>Mann-Whitney U</td></tr><tr><td>Numeric</td><td>same units measured twice (before/after)</td><td><strong>paired t-test</strong></td><td>Wilcoxon signed-rank</td></tr><tr><td>Numeric</td><td>3 or more groups</td><td>one-way <strong>ANOVA</strong> (then Tukey post-hoc)</td><td>Kruskal-Wallis</td></tr><tr><td>Binary proportions</td><td>two groups</td><td><strong>two-proportion z-test</strong> or chi-square</td><td>Fisher&#39;s exact (small counts)</td></tr><tr><td>Categorical vs categorical</td><td>contingency table</td><td><strong>chi-square test of independence</strong></td><td>Fisher&#39;s exact</td></tr><tr><td>Fit to a distribution</td><td>one sample</td><td>chi-square goodness of fit, Kolmogorov-Smirnov</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Normality</td><td>one sample</td><td>Shapiro-Wilk, QQ plot</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Equal variances</td><td>groups</td><td>Levene&#39;s test</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Association</td><td>two numerics</td><td>Pearson (linear) or Spearman (monotonic) correlation test</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>t-test assumptions:</strong> independent observations; the sampling distribution of the mean approximately normal (fine for large n even if the data is skewed); for Student&#39;s version, equal variances. Prefer <strong>Welch&#39;s</strong> version, which does not need equal variances.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-worked-examples-with-python\">Worked examples with Python</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> scipy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> stats\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np, pandas <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> pd\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 1. Paired t-test: same 8 users&#x27; weekly minutes before and after a feature</span>\nbefore = np.array([<span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">12</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">9</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">11</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">13</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">10</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">12</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">11</span>]); after = np.array([<span class=\"hljs-number\">11</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">14</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">9</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">13</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">14</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">12</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">13</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">12</span>])\nstats.ttest_rel(before, after)             <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># t = -5.0, p = 0.0016: usage rose. An independent test would ignore the pairing and lose power.</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 2. Two proportions: 90/500 converted in A, 130/500 in B</span>\ntable = np.array([[<span class=\"hljs-number\">90</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">410</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">130</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">370</span>]])\nchi2, p, dof, expected = stats.chi2_contingency(table, correction=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>)    <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># chi2 = 9.32, p = 0.0023, dof = 1</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># equivalent: proportions_ztest([90, 130], [500, 500])</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 3. Three groups: ANOVA, then a post-hoc test to see WHICH differ</span>\ng1, g2, g3 = [<span class=\"hljs-number\">5.1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">4.9</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5.6</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5.2</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">6.0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">6.3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5.8</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">6.1</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">5.0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5.4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5.3</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">5.1</span>]\nstats.f_oneway(g1, g2, g3)                 <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># F = 17.7, p = 0.0008</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># from statsmodels.stats.multicomp import pairwise_tukeyhsd   -&gt; controls error across the pairwise comparisons</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 4. Non-normal, small samples: rank-based test</span>\na = [<span class=\"hljs-number\">12.1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">11.8</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">13.4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">12.9</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">12.2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">13.0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">12.6</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">11.9</span>]; b = [<span class=\"hljs-number\">13.5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">14.1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">13.2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">14.8</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">13.9</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">14.4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">13.7</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">14.0</span>]\nstats.mannwhitneyu(a, b)                   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># p = 0.0003</span>\nstats.shapiro(a)                           <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># p = 0.60: no evidence against normality (but n = 8 cannot detect much)</span></code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Why not many t-tests instead of ANOVA?</strong> Three groups require three pairwise tests; at α = 0.05 each, the chance of at least one false positive is 1 − 0.95³ ≈ 14%. ANOVA tests &quot;any difference?&quot; with a single test; then a post-hoc test (Tukey) controls the family-wise error.</p>\n<p><strong>Normality tests with big samples.</strong> With thousands of rows, Shapiro-Wilk will reject for tiny, harmless deviations. Use a QQ plot and think about whether the CLT covers you instead of acting on the p-value alone.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-scenario-drill\">Scenario drill</h3>\n<p>Try each one before revealing.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-which-test~1","q":"Does the average order value differ between Android and iOS users (each user contributes one average; 5,000 users per group, somewhat skewed)?","a":"<p>Two independent numeric groups with large n: <strong>Welch&#39;s two-sample t-test</strong> (the CLT handles moderate skew). If the metric is very heavy-tailed, cap outliers or use a bootstrap for the difference in means, or <strong>Mann-Whitney U</strong> if you want a rank-based comparison. State that you compare means, which suits revenue totals.</p>\n","s":"Two independent groups, numeric: Welch&#39;s t-test (n is large, so skew is fine), or Mann-Whitney if you care about medians."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-which-test~2","q":"The same 200 customers are surveyed for satisfaction before and after a support redesign. Which test?","a":"<p>The observations are <strong>paired</strong> (same people twice), so analyse the within-person differences: a <strong>paired t-test</strong> on the differences (Wilcoxon signed-rank if the differences are skewed or ordinal). An independent test would ignore the pairing and lose power.</p>\n","s":"Paired t-test (or Wilcoxon signed-rank if the differences are far from normal)."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-which-test~3","q":"Is the choice of payment method (UPI, card, COD) related to whether an order is cancelled?","a":"<p>Both variables are categorical, so use the <strong>chi-square test of independence</strong> on the payment × cancelled table (expected counts should be at least about 5 per cell; otherwise use Fisher&#39;s exact test or merge categories). A significant result says they are associated; follow up with cell residuals or cancellation rates per method to say <em>how</em>.</p>\n","s":"Chi-square test of independence on the 3 × 2 contingency table."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-which-test~4","q":"You run an A/B/C test on three checkout designs with conversion as a binary outcome. What do you do?","a":"<p>First an <strong>omnibus chi-square test</strong> of independence for the 3 × 2 table (is conversion related to design?). If significant, run pairwise <strong>two-proportion z-tests</strong> with a correction for three comparisons (Holm or Bonferroni). Report each lift with a confidence interval, and decide the winner considering effect size and guardrail metrics.</p>\n","s":"Chi-square test across the 3 × 2 table, then pairwise two-proportion tests with a multiple-comparison correction."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-which-test~5","q":"Delivery-time samples from 10 orders in each of two cities are strongly right-skewed. Which test?","a":"<p>With n = 10 per group and strong skew, the t-test&#39;s normality assumption is doubtful and the CLT cannot rescue you. Use <strong>Mann-Whitney U</strong>, or log-transform the times (often roughly normal) and run Welch&#39;s t-test, or a <strong>permutation/bootstrap test</strong> on the median difference. Mention that the rank-based test compares distributions (stochastic dominance), not strictly means.</p>\n","s":"Mann-Whitney U (or a log transform followed by a t-test, or a permutation/bootstrap test)."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-which-test~6","q":"You want to know whether a model&#39;s prediction errors have the same variance in two customer segments.","a":"<p>Use <strong>Levene&#39;s test</strong> for equality of variances (robust to non-normality); the <strong>F-test</strong> is sensitive to non-normality. This is the usual check behind choosing equal- vs unequal-variance t-tests, though Welch&#39;s test makes it largely unnecessary.</p>\n","s":"Levene&#39;s test (robust to non-normality) or an F-test if errors are normal."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-which-test~ca0_1","q":"Conversion in control 10% versus treatment 11%, large samples. Which test?","a":"<p>Two-proportion z-test (or chi-square on the 2×2 table).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-which-test~ca0_2","q":"Same users measured before and after a change. Why paired?","a":"<p>Pairing removes between-user variance, so it is more powerful and respects the dependence.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-which-test~ca0_3","q":"Three variants of a page, mean time on page: first step?","a":"<p>ANOVA (or Kruskal-Wallis), then post-hoc pairwise tests with correction if significant.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m5-which-test~ta1_1","q":"<p>Name the test: (a) the share of users who click, for two subject lines; (b) average order value for the same 50 customers before and after a loyalty programme; (c) whether the city (4 cities) and the preferred payment method (3 methods) are related.</p>\n","a":"<p>(a) Two independent proportions: a two-proportion z-test or chi-square. (b) A number, paired (the same customers): a paired t-test on the per-customer differences; with heavy outliers, the Wilcoxon signed-rank test. (c) Two categorical variables in a 4 × 3 table: a chi-square test of independence.</p>\n"}]},"m5-multiple-testing":{"id":"m5-multiple-testing","slug":"multiple-testing-and-python-recipes","m":5,"title":"Multiple testing and Python recipes","min":30,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m5-which-test"],"tags":["statistics","hypothesis-testing","python"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"The multiple-testing problem and its corrections, plus a cookbook of Python recipes: t-tests, chi-square, proportion tests, bootstrap confidence intervals and permutation tests.","keyTerms":["multiple comparisons","Bonferroni","Holm","Benjamini-Hochberg","false discovery rate","bootstrap","permutation test"],"interviewQs":["What is the multiple testing problem and how do you correct for it?","Bonferroni vs Benjamini-Hochberg?","How do you compute a confidence interval when no formula is easy?"],"mistakes":["Testing many segments without correcting","Using Bonferroni when hundreds of tests make it too conservative","Bootstrapping dependent data naively"],"practice":"Simulate 1,000 tests with no real effect at alpha 0.05 and count the false positives before and after correction.","related":["m5-which-test","m6-pitfalls"],"points":"Running m tests inflates false positives: P(at least one FP) = 1 - (1 - alpha)^m. Fixes: Bonferroni (alpha/m; conservative), Holm, Benjamini-Hochberg (controls False Discovery Rate)."},"intuitive":"<p>If you flip a coin ten times and look for one lucky streak, you will probably find one. Testing one idea at the 5% level is fair, but testing twenty ideas means you will probably &quot;discover&quot; something that is just luck. Corrections make the bar higher when you ask many questions: Bonferroni divides the allowed error among all tests (strict), while Benjamini-Hochberg controls the proportion of false discoveries among the things you call discoveries (more forgiving).</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Dashboards that slice experiments by many segments and metrics create false wins unless you correct or label them exploratory.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> More looks mean more false alarms. Correct, or treat extra findings as hypotheses.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;If I run many tests, some will come out significant by chance. At five per cent, twenty tests give about a sixty-four per cent chance of at least one false positive. Bonferroni divides alpha by the number of tests, simple but strict. Benjamini-Hochberg controls the false discovery rate, the expected share of false findings, and is better when I run hundreds of tests. Better still is to decide the primary metric before the experiment. In Python, scipy gives the tests and statsmodels gives multipletests. The catch: slicing results into many segments after the fact is the same problem, so such findings are hypotheses to test again.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"Twenty metrics and the false alarm you are bound to find","html":"<p>An experiment changes nothing, but you test 20 metrics at α = 0.05.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Expected false alarms:</strong> 20 × 0.05 = <strong>1</strong>. You should expect one &quot;significant&quot; metric by pure chance.</li>\n<li><strong>Chance of at least one:</strong> 1 − 0.95^20 ≈ <strong>0.64</strong>. Most such experiments will show a &quot;winner&quot;.</li>\n<li><strong>Bonferroni:</strong> require p &lt; 0.05 / 20 = <strong>0.0025</strong> for each metric. Strict, but it keeps the chance of any false alarm under 5%.</li>\n<li><strong>Benjamini-Hochberg, on five p-values:</strong> 0.001, 0.012, 0.039, 0.041, 0.6 (already sorted). Compare the i-th smallest with (i / 5) × 0.05:</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>rank i</th><th>p-value</th><th>threshold (i/5) × 0.05</th><th>passes?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>0.001</td><td>0.01</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>0.012</td><td>0.02</td><td>yes</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>0.039</td><td>0.03</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>0.041</td><td>0.04</td><td>no</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>0.6</td><td>0.05</td><td>no</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ol start=\"5\">\n<li><strong>Read the table.</strong> The largest rank that passes is 2, so BH keeps the first two findings. Bonferroni (threshold 0.05 / 5 = 0.01) keeps only the first. BH controls the share of false discoveries instead of the chance of any, so it keeps more.</li>\n<li><strong>Best fix of all:</strong> decide one primary metric before the test starts.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> Test many things and something will look significant. Correct for it, or treat it as a lead for the next test.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"c-5-4-multiple-testing","text":"Multiple testing","level":2},{"id":"c-5-4-python-snippets","text":"Python snippets","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-how-fast-false-positives-pile-up","text":"How fast false positives pile up","level":3},{"id":"h-the-corrections","text":"The corrections","level":3},{"id":"h-python-cookbook","text":"Python cookbook","level":3},{"id":"h-bootstrap-a-ci-for-almost-any-statistic","text":"Bootstrap: a CI for almost any statistic","level":3},{"id":"h-permutation-test-a-test-with-no-distribution-ass","text":"Permutation test: a test with no distribution assumptions","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-5-4-multiple-testing\">Multiple testing</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<p>Running m tests inflates false positives: P(at least one FP) = 1 - (1 - alpha)^m. Fixes: Bonferroni (alpha/m; conservative), Holm, Benjamini-Hochberg (controls False Discovery Rate).</p>\n"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-5-4-python-snippets\">Python snippets</h2>"},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> scipy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> stats\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Two-sample Welch t-test</span>\nt, p = stats.ttest_ind(a, b, equal_var=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Paired t-test</span>\nt, p = stats.ttest_rel(before, after)\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Chi-square test of independence</span>\nchi2, p, dof, exp = stats.chi2_contingency(pd.crosstab(df.g, df.converted))\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Two-proportion z-test</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> statsmodels.stats.proportion <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> proportions_ztest\nz, p = proportions_ztest([conv_a, conv_b], [n_a, n_b])\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Bootstrap CI for the mean</span>\nrng = np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)\nboot = [rng.choice(x, size=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(x), replace=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>).mean() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> _ <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">10_000</span>)]\nci = np.percentile(boot, [<span class=\"hljs-number\">2.5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">97.5</span>])\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Normality</span>\nstats.shapiro(x)</code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-how-fast-false-positives-pile-up\">How fast false positives pile up</h3>\n<p>If every null is true and each test uses α = 0.05, the chance of at least one false positive in m independent tests is 1 − (1 − α)ᵐ:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>m tests</th><th>1</th><th>5</th><th>10</th><th>20</th><th>100</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>P(at least one false positive)</td><td>5%</td><td>23%</td><td>40%</td><td>64%</td><td>99.4%</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-the-corrections\">The corrections</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Bonferroni:</strong> use α/m for each test (for 20 tests, 0.0025). Simple, controls the family-wise error rate (FWER), but conservative and loses power as m grows.</li>\n<li><strong>Holm:</strong> a step-down version that is uniformly more powerful than Bonferroni with the same FWER guarantee. Sort p-values, compare the smallest with α/m, the next with α/(m−1), and so on, stopping at the first failure.</li>\n<li><strong>Benjamini-Hochberg (BH):</strong> controls the <strong>false discovery rate</strong> (the expected share of false discoveries among rejections). Sort p-values, find the largest k with p₍ₖ₎ ≤ (k/m) α, and reject the k smallest. The standard choice when screening many hypotheses (genes, features, segments).</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> statsmodels.stats.multitest <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> multipletests\npvals = [<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.001</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.008</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.012</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.03</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.04</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.5</span>]\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> m <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> (<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;bonferroni&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;holm&quot;</span>, <span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;fdr_bh&quot;</span>):\n    rejected, adjusted, _, _ = multipletests(pvals, alpha=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.05</span>, method=m)\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(m, rejected.tolist())\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># bonferroni [True, False, False, False, False, False, False]    1 discovery</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># holm       [True, True,  False, False, False, False, False]    2 discoveries</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># fdr_bh     [True, True,  True,  False, False, False, False]    3 discoveries   (most permissive)</span></code></pre></div>\n<p><strong>Practical advice.</strong> Pre-register one primary metric; treat the rest as secondary or guardrails; correct segment-level analyses; label exploratory findings as hypotheses to confirm in a follow-up experiment.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-python-cookbook\">Python cookbook</h3>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> scipy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> stats\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">from</span> statsmodels.stats.proportion <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> proportions_ztest, proportion_confint\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Welch t-test, paired t-test, chi-square (see the previous topic for outputs)</span>\nt, p = stats.ttest_ind(a, b, equal_var=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">False</span>)\nt, p = stats.ttest_rel(before, after)\nchi2, p, dof, exp = stats.chi2_contingency(pd.crosstab(df.group, df.converted))\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Two-proportion z-test: 1,440/14,400 (10%) vs 1,584/14,400 (11%)</span>\nz, p = proportions_ztest([<span class=\"hljs-number\">1440</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1584</span>], [<span class=\"hljs-number\">14400</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">14400</span>])       <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># z = -2.77, p = 0.0056</span>\nproportion_confint(<span class=\"hljs-number\">120</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">1000</span>, method=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;normal&quot;</span>)                <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># (0.0999, 0.1401): 12% +/- 2 points</span>\n\n<span class=\"hljs-comment\"># Normality</span>\nstats.shapiro(x)</code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-bootstrap-a-ci-for-almost-any-statistic\">Bootstrap: a CI for almost any statistic</h3>\n<p>Resample the data with replacement many times, compute the statistic each time, and take percentiles of the results.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">rng = np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)\nx = np.array([<span class=\"hljs-number\">12.1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">11.8</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">13.4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">12.9</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">12.2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">13.0</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">12.6</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">11.9</span>])\nboot = [rng.choice(x, size=<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(x), replace=<span class=\"hljs-literal\">True</span>).mean() <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> _ <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">10_000</span>)]\nnp.percentile(boot, [<span class=\"hljs-number\">2.5</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">97.5</span>])        <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># about [12.1, 12.9]: the 95% bootstrap CI for the mean</span></code></pre></div>\n<p>It works for medians, ratios and model metrics where no simple formula exists. It is <strong>not</strong> appropriate for dependent data unless you resample blocks (time series) or clusters (users), and it struggles for extreme statistics like the maximum.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-permutation-test-a-test-with-no-distribution-ass\">Permutation test: a test with no distribution assumptions</h3>\n<p>If the group labels do not matter under H₀, shuffling them should give similar differences. Count how often a shuffled difference is as extreme as the observed one:</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">obs = b.mean() - a.mean()\npool = np.concatenate([a, b]); count = <span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> _ <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">20_000</span>):\n    rng.shuffle(pool)\n    count += <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">abs</span>(pool[:<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(a)].mean() - pool[<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">len</span>(a):].mean()) &gt;= <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">abs</span>(obs)\np_value = (count + <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>) / (<span class=\"hljs-number\">20_000</span> + <span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>)       <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># about 0.0003 for the delivery-time example</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-multiple-testing~1","q":"You ran an experiment and look at conversion in 12 segments (city, device and so on); two segments show p &lt; 0.05. What do you conclude?","a":"<p>At α = 0.05, 12 independent looks give a 46% chance of at least one false positive and about 0.6 expected false positives. So two &quot;significant&quot; segments are weak evidence. Apply Holm or BH across the 12, check that the effect is large and plausible (and was hypothesised before), and confirm in a follow-up experiment. Also be wary of <strong>subgroup</strong> analysis when the overall effect is flat.</p>\n","s":"With 12 segment looks, about 0.6 false positives are expected by chance; treat them as hypotheses unless they survive correction."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-multiple-testing~ca0_1","q":"You test 20 independent metrics at α = 0.05 with no true effects. Chance of at least one &#39;significant&#39;?","a":"<p>1 - 0.95^20 ≈ 64%.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-multiple-testing~ca0_2","q":"Bonferroni versus Benjamini-Hochberg?","a":"<p>Bonferroni controls the chance of any false positive (strict); BH controls the false discovery rate (more power when testing many).</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-multiple-testing~ca0_3","q":"An A/B test shows no overall effect, but one of 12 segments is significant (p = 0.04). Ship it?","a":"<p>No: with 12 slices a hit is expected by chance; treat it as a hypothesis and confirm in a new test.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m5-multiple-testing~ta1_1","q":"<p>A team slices an A/B test into 12 segments and finds one with p = 0.03. Using Bonferroni, is it significant at an overall 5% level? What should they do?</p>\n","a":"<p>The Bonferroni threshold is 0.05 / 12 ≈ 0.0042, and 0.03 is far above it, so it is not significant once you account for 12 looks. With 12 tries, the chance of at least one p below 0.05 by luck is 1 − 0.95^12 ≈ 46%. Treat the segment as a hypothesis: write it down and test it in a fresh experiment designed for that segment.</p>\n"}]},"m5-other":{"id":"m5-other","slug":"other-essentials-sampling-mle-bayesian-linear-algebra-calcul","m":5,"title":"Other essentials: sampling, MLE, Bayesian, linear algebra, calculus","min":40,"type":"learn","priority":"Highest","prereq":[],"tags":["statistics","mathematics"],"qa":5,"ai":{"scope":"Essential supporting concepts: sampling methods and biases, maximum likelihood, Bayesian vs frequentist thinking, and the linear algebra and calculus used in machine learning.","keyTerms":["sampling methods","bias","MLE","Bayesian vs frequentist","prior","posterior","bootstrap","linear algebra","eigenvectors","cosine similarity","gradient descent"],"interviewQs":["Explain maximum likelihood estimation.","Bayesian vs frequentist: what is the difference in practice?","What are eigenvectors and why do they matter for PCA?","Explain gradient descent using derivatives."],"mistakes":["Treating a convenience sample as representative","Saying MLE and MAP are the same","Memorising linear algebra terms without intuition"],"practice":"Derive the MLE of a coin's bias by hand, then update a Beta prior with the same data and compare.","related":["m7-optimization","m8-dim-reduction","m5-hypothesis"],"points":"- Sampling methods: simple random, stratified, cluster, systematic; bias types (selection, survivorship, non-response, confirmation).\n- Bias vs variance of an estimator; MLE (conceptual): choose parameters that maximize the likelihood of observed data. Linear regression with Gaussian noise equals MLE; cross-entropy loss equals negative log-likelihood of Bernoulli.\n- Bayesian vs frequentist: frequentist treats parameters as fixed, probability as long-run frequency; Bayesian treats parameters as random with priors updated to posteriors. Bayesian A/B testing gives P(B > A) directly.\n- Bootstrapping: resample with replacement to estimate sampling distributions when formulas are hard.\n- Linear algebra you must know: vectors, dot product, matrix multiplication, transpose, inverse, rank, eigenvalues/eigenvectors (PCA), SVD (conceptual), norms (L1, L2), cosine similarity.\n- Calculus you must know: derivative as slope, partial derivatives, gradient, chain rule (backprop), gradient descent update: theta := theta - lr * grad."},"intuitive":"<p>Four toolbox ideas sit underneath everything else. <strong>Sampling</strong>: how you pick people decides whether your data is fair. <strong>Maximum likelihood</strong>: choose the settings that make the data you actually saw the least surprising. <strong>Bayesian thinking</strong>: start with a belief, see data, update the belief. <strong>Maths for ML</strong>: vectors are lists of numbers with direction, matrices transform them, and the derivative tells you which way is downhill, which is how models learn.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> These ideas explain why models are trained the way they are, and interviewers probe them for depth.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Sample fairly, fit by likelihood, update beliefs with data, and use derivatives to descend.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;A few more ideas come up often. Sampling: simple random, stratified to guarantee each group is represented, and cluster. Selection and survivorship bias distort samples. Maximum likelihood chooses the parameters that make the observed data most probable; for a normal it gives the sample mean. Bayesian thinking updates a prior with data into a posterior, giving probability statements about parameters. Linear algebra: a matrix transforms vectors, eigenvectors keep direction under that transform, and PCA uses them of the covariance matrix. Calculus: the gradient points uphill, so gradient descent steps the opposite way. The catch: frequentist intervals and Bayesian credible intervals answer slightly different questions.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[{"title":"The maths you need, in plain words","html":"<p>Five tools appear throughout data science. Each one, with a number you can check.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Σ (sigma) means &quot;add them up&quot;.</strong> Σ xᵢ for x = 2, 4, 6 is 2 + 4 + 6 = 12. The mean is (1/n) Σ xᵢ = 12 / 3 = 4.</li>\n<li><strong>A logarithm undoes a power.</strong> log₁₀(1,000) = 3 because 10³ = 1,000. The natural log uses e ≈ 2.718: ln(e²) = 2. Logs turn multiplication into addition and squash long tails, which is why we take log(income).</li>\n<li><strong>A derivative is a slope.</strong> For f(x) = x², the slope at x = 3 is 2 × 3 = 6. Check it numerically: (3.01² − 3²) / 0.01 = 6.01. Gradient descent uses slopes to know which way is downhill.</li>\n<li><strong>A dot product adds up products.</strong> [1, 2] · [3, 4] = 1 × 3 + 2 × 4 = 11. A linear model&#39;s prediction is a dot product of weights and features, plus a bias.</li>\n<li><strong>Maximum likelihood picks the most believable parameter.</strong> A coin gives 7 heads in 10. The likelihood of that result is p⁷(1 − p)³. At p = 0.5 it is 0.00098; at p = 0.6 it is 0.00179; at p = 0.7 it is 0.00222, the highest. The best estimate is p = 7/10, the observed share.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The notation is shorthand for simple arithmetic. When a formula looks frightening, put in three small numbers and compute it by hand.</p>\n"}],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"worked-example","text":"Worked example","level":2},{"id":"your-turn","text":"Your turn","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-sampling-and-bias","text":"Sampling and bias","level":3},{"id":"h-estimators-bias-and-variance","text":"Estimators: bias and variance","level":3},{"id":"h-maximum-likelihood-estimation-mle","text":"Maximum likelihood estimation (MLE)","level":3},{"id":"h-bayesian-vs-frequentist","text":"Bayesian vs frequentist","level":3},{"id":"h-bootstrap-in-one-sentence","text":"Bootstrap, in one sentence","level":3},{"id":"h-linear-algebra-for-ml","text":"Linear algebra for ML","level":3},{"id":"h-calculus-for-ml","text":"Calculus for ML","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<ul>\n<li><strong>Sampling methods:</strong> simple random, stratified, cluster, systematic; bias types (selection, survivorship, non-response, confirmation).</li>\n<li><strong>Bias vs variance of an estimator; MLE (conceptual):</strong> choose parameters that maximize the likelihood of observed data. Linear regression with Gaussian noise equals MLE; cross-entropy loss equals negative log-likelihood of Bernoulli.</li>\n<li><strong>Bayesian vs frequentist:</strong> frequentist treats parameters as fixed, probability as long-run frequency; Bayesian treats parameters as random with priors updated to posteriors. Bayesian A/B testing gives P(B &gt; A) directly.</li>\n<li><strong>Bootstrapping:</strong> resample with replacement to estimate sampling distributions when formulas are hard.</li>\n<li><strong>Linear algebra you must know:</strong> vectors, dot product, matrix multiplication, transpose, inverse, rank, eigenvalues/eigenvectors (PCA), SVD (conceptual), norms (L1, L2), cosine similarity.</li>\n<li><strong>Calculus you must know:</strong> derivative as slope, partial derivatives, gradient, chain rule (backprop), gradient descent update: <code>theta := theta - lr * grad</code>.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-sampling-and-bias\">Sampling and bias</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>How</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Simple random</td><td>every unit equally likely</td><td>the baseline</td></tr><tr><td>Stratified</td><td>sample within groups (strata)</td><td>guarantees small groups are represented; lowers variance</td></tr><tr><td>Cluster</td><td>sample whole groups (stores, cities)</td><td>cheaper logistics; needs cluster-aware SEs</td></tr><tr><td>Systematic</td><td>every k-th item</td><td>simple; beware periodic patterns</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p><strong>Biases to name:</strong> <em>selection bias</em> (who enters the data is not random), <em>survivorship bias</em> (only successes are visible, such as analysing only companies that still exist), <em>non-response bias</em> (responders differ from non-responders), <em>confirmation bias</em> (seeing what you expect).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-estimators-bias-and-variance\">Estimators: bias and variance</h3>\n<p>An estimator&#39;s <strong>bias</strong> is how far its average is from the truth; its <strong>variance</strong> is how much it fluctuates. Sample mean: unbiased. Sample variance with n − 1: unbiased. Low bias and low variance are both wanted; sometimes accepting a little bias reduces variance enough to lower total error (this is exactly what regularisation does).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-maximum-likelihood-estimation-mle\">Maximum likelihood estimation (MLE)</h3>\n<p>Pick the parameters that maximise the <strong>likelihood</strong>, the probability of the observed data. Example: a coin shows 7 heads in 10 flips. Likelihood of bias p: L(p) = C(10,7) p⁷ (1−p)³. The maximum (set the derivative of log L to zero) is at <strong>p̂ = 7/10</strong>, the sample proportion. For a normal distribution the MLE of the mean is the sample mean and of the variance is the <strong>population-style</strong> variance (divide by n, slightly biased).</p>\n<p>Why it matters in ML: <strong>squared-error loss is the negative log-likelihood of Gaussian noise</strong>, and <strong>cross-entropy (log loss) is the negative log-likelihood of Bernoulli labels</strong>. Training by minimising these losses <em>is</em> maximum likelihood, which is why linear regression with Gaussian errors equals least squares and logistic regression is fit by gradient descent on log loss. Adding a prior gives <strong>MAP</strong> estimation, which corresponds to regularisation (Gaussian prior ↔ L2, Laplace prior ↔ L1).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-bayesian-vs-frequentist\">Bayesian vs frequentist</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Frequentist</th><th>Bayesian</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Parameters</td><td>fixed, unknown</td><td>random, with a prior distribution</td></tr><tr><td>Probability means</td><td>long-run frequency</td><td>degree of belief</td></tr><tr><td>Output</td><td>estimate, CI, p-value</td><td>posterior distribution, credible interval</td></tr><tr><td>Uses prior knowledge</td><td>informally</td><td>explicitly via the prior</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>A small Bayesian example: a prior Beta(1, 1) (flat) for a conversion probability; observe 7 conversions and 3 failures; the posterior is <strong>Beta(8, 4)</strong>, with mean 0.667, a 95% credible interval of about (0.39, 0.89), and <strong>P(p &gt; 0.5) ≈ 0.89</strong>. This direct statement (&quot;an 89% chance the rate exceeds 50%&quot;) is what makes Bayesian A/B testing easy to communicate: report P(B &gt; A) and the expected loss instead of a p-value.</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-bootstrap-in-one-sentence\">Bootstrap, in one sentence</h3>\n<p>Resample your data with replacement to approximate the sampling distribution of any statistic when formulas are hard (see the cookbook in the previous topic).</p>\n<h3 id=\"h-linear-algebra-for-ml\">Linear algebra for ML</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Vector</strong> as a list of numbers (a point or a direction); <strong>dot product</strong> <code>a·b = Σ aᵢbᵢ</code> measures alignment; <strong>norms</strong>: L1 = Σ|xᵢ|, L2 = √Σxᵢ².</li>\n<li><strong>Cosine similarity</strong> = <code>a·b / (‖a‖ ‖b‖)</code> measures the angle between vectors, ignoring length; it powers text and embedding similarity.</li>\n<li><strong>Matrix multiplication</strong> composes linear transformations: a layer of a neural network is <code>W x + b</code>. <strong>Transpose</strong> flips rows and columns; the <strong>inverse</strong> undoes a transformation (exists only for square, non-singular matrices); <strong>rank</strong> is the number of independent directions.</li>\n<li><strong>Eigenvectors</strong> of a matrix are directions that the matrix only stretches (by the eigenvalue). For the covariance matrix of data, the eigenvectors are the <strong>principal components</strong> (directions of maximum variance) and the eigenvalues say how much variance each explains: that is <strong>PCA</strong>. <strong>SVD</strong> generalises this to any matrix and is used in recommenders and dimensionality reduction.</li>\n</ul>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\"><span class=\"hljs-keyword\">import</span> numpy <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">as</span> np\nu, v = np.array([<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>]), np.array([<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">4</span>, <span class=\"hljs-number\">6.5</span>])\nu @ v / (np.linalg.norm(u) * np.linalg.norm(v))      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 0.9993  nearly the same direction</span>\n\nX = np.array([[<span class=\"hljs-number\">2.5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2.4</span>],[<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.7</span>],[<span class=\"hljs-number\">2.2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2.9</span>],[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1.9</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2.2</span>],[<span class=\"hljs-number\">3.1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">3.0</span>],[<span class=\"hljs-number\">2.3</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">2.7</span>],[<span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1.6</span>],[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1.1</span>],[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1.5</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">1.6</span>],[<span class=\"hljs-number\">1.1</span>,<span class=\"hljs-number\">0.9</span>]])\nXc = X - X.mean(axis=<span class=\"hljs-number\">0</span>)\nvals, vecs = np.linalg.eigh(np.cov(Xc.T))\nvals[::-<span class=\"hljs-number\">1</span>] / vals.<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">sum</span>()                              <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># [0.963, 0.037]: one component explains 96% of the variance</span></code></pre></div>\n<h3 id=\"h-calculus-for-ml\">Calculus for ML</h3>\n<p>The <strong>derivative</strong> is the slope of a function; the <strong>gradient</strong> is the vector of partial derivatives, pointing uphill. The <strong>chain rule</strong> (derivative of a function of a function) is what <strong>backpropagation</strong> applies layer by layer. <strong>Gradient descent</strong> steps against the gradient: <code>θ ← θ − η ∇L(θ)</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"code\"><div class=\"code-h\"><span>Python</span><button class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Copy code to clipboard\">Copy</button></div><pre tabindex=\"0\"><code class=\"hljs\">x = <span class=\"hljs-number\">5.0</span>                      <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># minimise f(x) = x^2, gradient 2x, learning rate 0.1</span>\n<span class=\"hljs-keyword\">for</span> _ <span class=\"hljs-keyword\">in</span> <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">range</span>(<span class=\"hljs-number\">5</span>):\n    x -= <span class=\"hljs-number\">0.1</span> * <span class=\"hljs-number\">2</span> * x\n    <span class=\"hljs-built_in\">print</span>(<span class=\"hljs-built_in\">round</span>(x, <span class=\"hljs-number\">3</span>), end=<span class=\"hljs-string\">&quot; &quot;</span>)   <span class=\"hljs-comment\"># 4.0 3.2 2.56 2.048 1.638   each step shrinks x by 20%</span></code></pre></div>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-other~1","q":"You estimate the conversion rate from 1 success in 5 trials. What is the MLE, and why might you not trust it?","a":"<p>The MLE is the sample proportion, 1/5 = <strong>0.2</strong>. With five trials the estimate is extremely uncertain (a 95% interval spans roughly 0.01 to 0.72). A Bayesian estimate with a sensible prior (for example Beta(2, 18) reflecting a typical 10% rate) shrinks it toward prior knowledge: the posterior mean is (2 + 1) / (2 + 18 + 5) = 0.12. This &quot;shrinkage&quot; is also what regularisation does.</p>\n","s":"MLE = 0.2, but with so little data it has a huge variance; a prior (Bayesian or regularised estimate) is more sensible."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-other~ca0_1","q":"When is stratified sampling better than simple random sampling?","a":"<p>When key subgroups are small or differ a lot; it guarantees representation and reduces variance.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-other~ca0_2","q":"What does the gradient tell gradient descent?","a":"<p>The direction of steepest increase of the loss; the algorithm steps in the opposite direction.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-other~ca0_3","q":"How do a 95% confidence interval and a 95% credible interval differ?","a":"<p>Confidence: 95% of intervals built this way would cover the true value over repeats. Credible: given the data and prior, there is 95% probability the parameter lies inside.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","tr":1,"id":"m5-other~ta1_1","q":"<p>Compute: (a) Σ (xᵢ − 4)² for x = 2, 4, 6; (b) the slope of f(x) = 3x² at x = 2; (c) the dot product of the weights [0.5, −1] with the features [4, 3], plus a bias of 2.</p>\n","a":"<p>(a) (−2)² + 0² + 2² = 8. Divided by n − 1 = 2, it is the sample variance, 4. (b) The slope of 3x² is 6x, so at x = 2 it is 12. Check: (3 × 2.01² − 3 × 4) / 0.01 ≈ 12.03. (c) 0.5 × 4 + (−1) × 3 + 2 = 2 − 3 + 2 = 1. That is exactly how a linear model makes a prediction.</p>\n"}]},"m5-qa":{"id":"m5-qa","slug":"stats-q-a-and-self-check","m":5,"title":"Stats Q&A and self-check","min":25,"type":"revise","priority":"Highest","prereq":["m5-hypothesis"],"tags":["statistics","interview-questions"],"qa":18,"ai":{"scope":"Short, well-structured answers to the most common statistics interview questions, with guidance on how to extend each answer.","keyTerms":["Bessel's correction","non-normal data","p-value misconceptions","statistical vs practical significance","power","outliers","correlation vs causation"],"interviewQs":["Why N-1 in sample variance?","Does a large p-value prove H0?","What affects power?","How do you handle outliers?"],"mistakes":["Giving one-line answers with no example","Forgetting to mention assumptions","Not distinguishing statistical and practical significance"],"practice":"Answer each question aloud in under 60 seconds, then reveal and compare.","related":["m5-hypothesis","m16-stats"],"points":"Q: Why N-1 in sample variance?\nA: The sample mean is estimated from the same data, so deviations are slightly too small; dividing by N-1 gives an unbiased estimate.\n\nQ: What if data is not normal?\nA: With large n CLT helps; otherwise use non-parametric tests, transformations, or bootstrap.\n\nQ: Does a large p-value prove H0?\nA: No; it means insufficient evidence against H0.\n\nQ: Statistical vs practical significance?\nA: With huge n, tiny effects become \"significant\"; always report effect size and CI.\n\nQ: What affects power?\nA: Effect size, sample size, alpha, variance (and one- vs two-tailed).\n\nQ: How to handle outliers?\nA: Investigate first; options are cap/winsorize, transform, robust models, or remove if an error.\n\nQ: Correlation vs causation?\nA: Confounders, reverse causality, coincidence; establish causation via randomized experiments or causal inference methods.\n\nQ: Mean vs median when to use?\nA: Median for skewed data (income, delivery time); mean when symmetric or when totals matter.\n\nQ: What is a confidence interval for a proportion?\nA: p_hat +/- z * sqrt(p_hat(1-p_hat)/n).\n\nQ: What is the odds ratio?\nA: Ratio of odds between groups; in logistic regression exp(coefficient).\n\nQ: What is heteroscedasticity?\nA: Non-constant error variance; affects standard errors, fix via log transform, robust SEs, WLS.\n\nQ: Difference between parametric and non-parametric?\nA: Parametric assume a distribution form (more power if true); non-parametric make fewer assumptions.\n\n- Explain CLT, p-value, and confidence interval to a layperson in under a minute each.\n- Solve the Bayes example without notes.\n- Given a scenario, choose the correct test and state assumptions."},"intuitive":"<p>This is where you test yourself on statistics. The questions repeat a few ideas: what a p-value means, why the mean can mislead, how sample size shrinks uncertainty, which test fits which data, and what goes wrong when you test too much. A good answer has three parts: a plain definition, a tiny example with numbers, and one trap to avoid. If you can say those in under a minute without notes, you own the topic. If you stumble, return to the matching section and say it again out loud.</p>\n<p class=\"why\"><strong>Why it matters:</strong> Interviewers probe statistics by asking you to explain, not compute.</p>\n<p class=\"take\"><strong>Takeaway:</strong> Definition, example, trap, in under a minute.</p>\n","explain":"<p>&quot;When asked a statistics question I answer in three beats. First a plain definition in one sentence, with no jargon. Second a small example with real numbers, such as a coin or a conversion rate. Third the trap, the usual misunderstanding. For instance, for the p-value: the chance of data this extreme if there were no effect; for example, p equals 0.03 on a ten per cent versus eleven per cent conversion test; the trap is that it is not the chance the null is true. Then I stop and invite follow-up. The catch: formulas help, but interviewers listen for whether I can state assumptions and limits.&quot;</p>\n","steps":[],"deeper":true,"outline":[{"id":"c-5-6","text":"5.6 Stats Q&amp;A (short answers)","level":2},{"id":"c-5-7","text":"5.7 Self-check (Module 5)","level":2},{"id":"go-deeper","text":"Go deeper","level":2},{"id":"h-the-answer-structure-that-works","text":"The answer structure that works","level":3},{"id":"h-more-questions-with-model-answers","text":"More questions with model answers","level":3},{"id":"check-yourself","text":"Check yourself","level":2}],"blocks":[{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-5-6\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.6</span> Stats Q&amp;A (short answers)</h2>"},{"k":"qa","id":"5.6#1","src":"c","q":"Why N-1 in sample variance?","a":"<p>The sample mean is estimated from the same data, so deviations are slightly too small; dividing by N-1 gives an unbiased estimate.</p>\n","s":"Deviations from the sample mean are slightly too small; dividing by n − 1 gives an unbiased variance estimate."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.6#2","src":"c","q":"What if data is not normal?","a":"<p>With large n CLT helps; otherwise use non-parametric tests, transformations, or bootstrap.</p>\n","s":"With large n the CLT helps; otherwise use non-parametric tests, transformations or the bootstrap."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.6#3","src":"c","q":"Does a large p-value prove H0?","a":"<p>No; it means insufficient evidence against H0.</p>\n","s":"No: it means insufficient evidence against H0, not proof that H0 is true."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.6#4","src":"c","q":"Statistical vs practical significance?","a":"<p>With huge n, tiny effects become &quot;significant&quot;; always report effect size and CI.</p>\n","s":"With huge n tiny effects become significant; always report effect size and a confidence interval."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.6#5","src":"c","q":"What affects power?","a":"<p>Effect size, sample size, alpha, variance (and one- vs two-tailed).</p>\n","s":"Effect size, sample size, significance level, variance (and one- versus two-tailed)."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.6#6","src":"c","q":"How to handle outliers?","a":"<p>Investigate first; options are cap/winsorize, transform, robust models, or remove if an error.</p>\n","s":"Investigate first; then cap or winsorise, transform, use robust methods, or remove if it is an error."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.6#7","src":"c","q":"Correlation vs causation?","a":"<p>Confounders, reverse causality, coincidence; establish causation via randomized experiments or causal inference methods.</p>\n","s":"Confounders, reverse causality and coincidence; use randomised experiments or causal methods."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.6#8","src":"c","q":"Mean vs median when to use?","a":"<p>Median for skewed data (income, delivery time); mean when symmetric or when totals matter.</p>\n","s":"Median for skewed data; mean when symmetric or when totals matter."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.6#9","src":"c","q":"What is a confidence interval for a proportion?","a":"<p>p_hat +/- z * sqrt(p_hat(1-p_hat)/n).</p>\n","s":"p̂ ± z × √(p̂(1 − p̂)/n)."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.6#10","src":"c","q":"What is the odds ratio?","a":"<p>Ratio of odds between groups; in logistic regression exp(coefficient).</p>\n","s":"The ratio of the odds between groups; in logistic regression it is exp(coefficient)."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.6#11","src":"c","q":"What is heteroscedasticity?","a":"<p>Non-constant error variance; affects standard errors, fix via log transform, robust SEs, WLS.</p>\n","s":"Non-constant error variance; it biases standard errors, fix with a log transform, robust SEs or weighted least squares."},{"k":"qa","id":"5.6#12","src":"c","q":"Difference between parametric and non-parametric?","a":"<p>Parametric assume a distribution form (more power if true); non-parametric make fewer assumptions.</p>\n","s":"Parametric tests assume a distribution form and are more powerful if it holds; non-parametric ones assume less."},{"k":"html","src":"c","html":"<h2 class=\"chunk-h\" id=\"c-5-7\"><span class=\"sec-num\">5.7</span> Self-check (Module 5)</h2>"},{"k":"cl","id":"m5-qa.c1","kind":"self","items":["Explain CLT, p-value, and confidence interval to a layperson in under a minute each.","Solve the Bayes example without notes.","Given a scenario, choose the correct test and state assumptions."]},{"k":"div","id":"go-deeper","label":"Go deeper: explained, with worked examples"},{"k":"html","src":"a","html":"<h3 id=\"h-the-answer-structure-that-works\">The answer structure that works</h3>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>One-sentence answer</strong> that a non-specialist can follow.</li>\n<li><strong>A concrete example</strong> (an A/B test, delivery times, churn).</li>\n<li><strong>A catch or assumption</strong> (when it fails, what to check).</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"h-more-questions-with-model-answers\">More questions with model answers</h3>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-qa~1","q":"Why do we use the median for income but the mean for total revenue?","a":"<p>For a skewed variable the median describes the typical unit; for totals the mean is essential because <code>total = mean × n</code>, and the median loses that relationship. Choose by the decision: &quot;what does a typical customer look like?&quot; (median) versus &quot;how much revenue should we plan for?&quot; (mean).</p>\n","s":"Income is right-skewed so the mean misrepresents the typical person; revenue is a sum, where the mean times the count gives the total."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-qa~2","q":"What is the difference between a confidence interval and a prediction interval?","a":"<p>A confidence interval quantifies uncertainty in an <strong>estimated parameter</strong> (the average order value) and shrinks as n grows. A <strong>prediction interval</strong> covers where a <strong>single future observation</strong> will fall; it includes both parameter uncertainty and the individual-level noise, so it stays wide however much data you have.</p>\n","s":"A CI is for a parameter (like the mean); a prediction interval is for a single new observation and is wider."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","id":"m5-qa~3","q":"What happens to a confidence interval if you double the sample size?","a":"<p>The width is proportional to σ/√n, so doubling n divides the width by √2 ≈ 1.41, a 29% reduction. To halve the interval you need four times the data, which is why precision gets expensive.</p>\n","s":"It narrows by a factor of √2 (about 29%), not by half."},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-qa~ca0_1","q":"Give the three-beat answer for &quot;what is a confidence interval?&quot;","a":"<p>Definition: a range built so that 95% of such ranges cover the true value. Example: conversion 10% ± 1% from 10k users. Trap: not a 95% probability that this particular interval holds the truth.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-qa~ca0_2","q":"How do you handle a statistics question you are unsure of?","a":"<p>State what you know, derive from first principles with a simple case, flag the assumption, and say how you would verify it. Never bluff.</p>\n"},{"k":"qa","src":"a","chk":1,"id":"m5-qa~ca0_3","q":"Which five stats ideas should you be able to explain from memory?","a":"<p>p-value, confidence interval, Type I/II error and power, CLT, and Bayes&#39; theorem with base rates.</p>\n"}]}});